window.REALTEST_DATA = {"id":7,"title":"Time Mode 2 · Dojo bổ sung","provider":"Tutorials Dojo","source":"DOJOS/Sections/TIME_MODE/tf2/📊 Your Progress - Tutorials Dojo.html","minutes":102,"supplementary":true,"questions":[{"id":1,"sourceNumber":1,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company is leveraging its extensive dataset to train a machine learning (ML) model for predictive analytics. The dataset includes sensitive personally identifiable information (PII), which should be excluded from the ML model. In compliance with security standards, the data also needs to be encrypted using AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS). A data engineer is tasked with choosing the right AWS services to prepare and provide this data to the ML model.</p>\n<p>Which combination of steps will meet these requirements in the MOST cost-effective manner? (Select TWO.)</p>","answers":["Deliver the data to an Amazon OpenSearch Service cluster encrypted with AWS KMS.","Store the data in an Amazon S3 bucket and enable security features with AWS KMS server-side encryption (SSE-KMS).","Use Amazon EMR to process and transform the data, applying custom scripts to mask PII before usage in the ML model.","Load the dataset into Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler to encode the PII, then proceed with model training.","Implement AWS Glue DataBrew to manage the data intake process and employ a Glue DataBrew recipe job to conceal the PII within the dataset."],"correct":[1,4],"explanation":"<p>AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) is a managed service that enables you to easily create and control cryptographic keys used to encrypt your data. These keys are used across a variety of AWS services to provide encryption of stored data.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/859153847226f4c519487e09cea766b26308acf37a2a6d98f923e21d1aaec025.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/c54b824a2333edb54f0465774b5e9efec2592ff47955ea4f90cf9146f6532068.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS (SSE-KMS) is a feature where AWS manages both the encryption process and the cryptographic keys. When you use Amazon S3 with SSE-KMS, for example, your data is automatically encrypted as it is written to S3 and decrypted when accessed, using keys managed in KMS. This process provides an additional layer of security by involving the management of encryption keys. <span><strong>This satisfies the encryption requirement of the problem.</strong></span></p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/79043dddc090e2729d156048e8a0929e8d01eb440460653daceb87c6afaa6d83.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>AWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that simplifies the process of cleaning and normalizing data. It features data masking capabilities, which are crucial for handling PII. DataBrew can be configured to identify and mask PII, ensuring that sensitive information is obscured or removed from datasets before they are used in machine learning models or analytics. This ensures compliance with regulations that mandate the protection of PII. This satisfies the PII masking requirement of the problem.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span><strong>- Store the data in an Amazon S3 bucket and enable security features with AWS KMS server-side encryption (SSE-KMS).</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>- Implement AWS Glue DataBrew to manage the data intake process and employ a Glue DataBrew recipe job to conceal the PII within the dataset.&nbsp;</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Deliver the data to an Amazon OpenSearch Service cluster encrypted with AWS KMS</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect. Although Amazon OpenSearch Service with AWS KMS encryption ensures data security, it is more costly due to its search and analytics capabilities, which are not necessary for simply delivering data to an ML model.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Use Amazon EMR to process and transform the data, applying custom scripts to mask PII before usage in the ML model </span></strong>is incorrect. Although Amazon EMR is capable of managing large datasets and executing custom scripts for tasks like PII masking, its cost-effectiveness is diminished due to its design for more extensive big data processing, analytics, and complex ETL jobs. These advanced capabilities of Amazon EMR exceed the simple requirements of PII masking for machine learning models. As a result, it becomes a less cost-efficient choice compared to more specialized data preparation tools, such as AWS Glue DataBrew, which are tailored for simpler data processing tasks without the additional features and associated costs of handling large-scale data processing.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Load the dataset into Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler to encode the PII, then proceed with model training</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect because this solution would not satisfy the requirement that the ML model should not use the PII. Encoding PII within Data Wrangler does not eliminate it. Instead, it merely alters its format, leaving the underlying sensitive information accessible and potentially usable by the ML model, which poses a risk of non-compliance with data privacy standards.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/bucket-encryption.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/bucket-encryption.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch%20service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-gs.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-gs.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/data-wrangler.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/data-wrangler.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out the TutorialsDojo Cheat Sheets here:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-key-management-service-aws-kms/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-key-management-service-aws-kms/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":2,"sourceNumber":3,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company uses a Lambda function to retrieve objects from an S3 bucket in the same AWS account. The current IAM policy for the Lambda execution role is as follows:<code><br>\n{<br>\n\"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",<br>\n\"Statement\": [<br>\n{<br>\n\"Effect\": \"Allow\",<br>\n\"Action\": [<br>\n\"s3:Get*\",<br>\n\"s3:Put*\"<br>\n],<br>\n\"Resource\": \"*\"<br>\n}<br>\n]<br>\n}<br>\n</code></p>\n<p>This policy is overly permissive and does not adhere to the principle of least privilege.</p>\n<p>The company needs to modify the IAM policy to ensure that the Lambda function has only the necessary permissions to interact with the S3 bucket.</p>\n<p>Which of the following modifications would be the most appropriate?</p>","answers":["<span>Replace the Action field with <code>\"Action\": [\"s3:PutObject\"]</code> and the Resource field with <code>\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*\"]</code>.</span>","<span>Add <code>\"Action\": [\"s3:ListBucket\"]</code> to the Action field. Replace the Resource field with <code>\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name\"]</code>.</span>","<span>Replace the Action field with <code>\"Action\": [\"s3:GetObject\"]</code> and the Resource field with <code>\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*\"]</code>.</span>","<span>Add the <code>Action\": [\"s3:GetObjectVersion\"]</code> to the Action field. Replace the Resource field with <code>\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*\"]</code>.</span>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) is a web service that helps you securely control access to AWS resources. When a&nbsp;principal&nbsp;makes a request in AWS, the AWS enforcement code checks whether the principal is authenticated (signed in) and authorized (has permissions). You manage access in AWS by creating policies and attaching them to IAM identities or AWS resources. Policies are JSON documents in AWS that, when attached to an identity or resource, define their permissions.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/f63f490b2330db9728393df9506be45e3b9542714e207f2c1679d15be1f5d0d3.png\"></p>\n<p>The&nbsp;<code>s3:GetObject</code>&nbsp;action grants permission to retrieve objects from an S3 bucket. This action adheres to the principle of least privilege, which is a security practice that restricts access rights for users to the bare minimum permissions they need to perform their work.</p>\n<p>When you specify the&nbsp;<code>s3:GetObject</code>&nbsp;action in the IAM policy, the Lambda function is allowed to retrieve objects from the specified S3 bucket. The&nbsp;<code>\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*\"]</code>&nbsp;part of the policy specifies the bucket and objects the policy applies to.</p>\n<p>This policy is appropriate for a Lambda function that needs to retrieve objects from an S3 bucket, as it grants only the necessary permissions and no more. This is in line with the principle of least privilege.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Replace the Action field with <code>\"Action\": [\"s3:GetObject\"]</code> and the Resource field with <code>\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*\"]</code></strong><strong>.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Replace the Action field with <code>\"Action\": [\"s3:PutObject\"]</code> and the Resource field with <code>\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*\"]</code></strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect because the&nbsp;<code>s3:PutObject</code>&nbsp;action allows only for writing or uploading objects to the S3 bucket, which is unnecessary for the Lambda function’s purpose of retrieving objects from the S3 bucket.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Add <code>\"Action\": [\"s3:ListBucket\"]</code> to the Action field. Replace the Resource field with <code>\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name\"]</code></strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect. While this is a step towards least privilege, it does not specifically allow retrieving objects with <code>s3:GetObject</code>, which is required for the Lambda function to perform its task. Moreover, <code>s3:ListBucket</code> is applied at the bucket level, not the object level, and does not alone fulfill the requirement to retrieve objects.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Add the <code>Action\": [\"s3:GetObjectVersion\"]</code> to the Action field. Replace the Resource field with <code>\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*\"]</code></strong></span><code></code>&nbsp;is incorrect. This policy would simply allow the function to retrieve different versions of an object in a versioned bucket. While this can be necessary for some use cases, it is not mentioned as a requirement in the scenario. Furthermore, the modification does not restrict actions to only those needed and includes additional permissions not specified as necessary.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/iam/resources/best-practices/\">https://aws.amazon.com/iam/resources/best-practices/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this&nbsp;AWS Identity and Access Management Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-identity-and-access-management-iam/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-identity-and-access-management-iam/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":3,"sourceNumber":4,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company is using AWS Glue for ETL jobs and Athena for querying data stored in S3. The corporation has strict security policies and requires fine-grained access control over AWS Glue resources.</p>\n<p>There are two departments in the corporation, Finance and HR. Each department has its own set of ETL jobs and databases in AWS Glue. The requirement is that the Finance department should only have access to run its own ETL jobs and query its own databases. The same applies to the HR department.</p>\n<p>Which of the following approaches would be the most appropriate to implement this requirement?</p>","answers":["Use Amazon S3 bucket policies to control access to AWS Glue resources.","Use AWS Glue Data Catalog encryption settings to control access to databases and ETL jobs.","Use AWS Glue Data Catalog resource policies to manage access at a granular level.","Use Amazon Athena workgroups to control access to AWS Glue resources."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>AWS Glue Catalog resource policies allow you to manage access at a granular level. You can specify permissions at the database, table, and column level. This is particularly useful in scenarios where you need to restrict access to specific databases or tables within AWS Glue, such as the scenario described in the question.</p>\n<p>In the given scenario, the corporation has two departments, Finance and HR, each with its own set of ETL jobs and databases in AWS Glue. The requirement is that each department should only have access to run its own ETL jobs and query its own databases. This level of access control can be achieved using AWS Glue Catalog resource policies.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/330d02045808e2d4ea6b80d95b4a9a2637f62523aefda8ed8862539ba00a6a8a.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>With AWS Glue Catalog resource policies, you can define fine-grained access to the AWS Glue data catalog by using resource-level permissions in IAM policies. These policies can restrict access to different portions of the catalog based on users, roles, or applied at a resource level. This allows you to provide granular control over which users can access the various metadata definitions in your data lake.</p>\n<p>In addition, it’s important to note that the AWS Glue Data Catalog policies define the access to the metadata, and the S3 policies define the access to the content itself. You can restrict which metadata operations can be performed, such as GetDatabases, GetTables, CreateTable, and others, using identity-based policies (IAM). You can also restrict which data catalog objects those operations are performed on.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Use AWS Glue Data Catalog resource policies to manage access at a granular level.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use Amazon S3 bucket policies to control access to AWS Glue resources</strong></span> is incorrect because Amazon S3 bucket policies are used to manage access to S3 buckets and objects, not AWS Glue resources.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use AWS Glue Data Catalog encryption settings to control access to databases and ETL jobs </strong></span>is incorrect because encryption settings are used for data security, not for managing access to AWS Glue resources.</p>\n<p>The option that says:<strong><span> Use Amazon Athena workgroups to control access to AWS Glue resources</span></strong> is incorrect because Amazon Athena workgroups are used to control access to Athena queries, not to control access to AWS Glue resources.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/fine-grained-access-to-glue-resources.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/fine-grained-access-to-glue-resources.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/restrict-access-to-your-aws-glue-data-catalog-with-resource-level-iam-permissions-and-resource-based-policies/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/restrict-access-to-your-aws-glue-data-catalog-with-resource-level-iam-permissions-and-resource-based-policies/</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":4,"sourceNumber":6,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company that uses AWS Lake Formation for its data lake solution is in the process of consolidating all marketing data into a centralized data lake. During the process, the team encounters a <code>NotAuthorized</code> error while trying to create a new database and data catalog within Lake Formation. This error hinders the team from properly managing the data lake and creating databases.</p>\n<p>What additional permissions, roles, or policies should the team be granted to resolve the issue? (Select TWO.)</p>","answers":["Data Lake Administrator","Database Creator","<span><code>IAMFullAccess</code></span>","Lake Formation Data Analyst","Lake Formation Data Catalog Full Access"],"correct":[0,1],"explanation":"<p>The Data Lake Administrator role is an important permission in AWS Lake Formation. This permission allows a user to perform several important tasks related to managing a data lake. These tasks include registering Amazon S3 locations, accessing the Data Catalog, creating databases, creating and running workflows, granting Lake Formation permissions to other users, and viewing AWS CloudTrail logs. Without this permission, a user would not be able to perform these tasks, which are essential for managing a data lake in AWS Lake Formation.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/96c3e3a708b1d5df25d044e42e673bd28400c7a8f9696c5cc8f088c49652fa55.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>The database creator permission is another important permission in AWS Lake Formation. This permission specifically allows a user to create new databases within the data lake. Creating databases is a fundamental task when managing a data lake, as databases are used to organize and store the data within the lake. Without this permission, a user would not be able to create new databases, which could hinder their ability to manage the data lake effectively.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p>-&nbsp;<span><strong>Data Lake Administrator</strong></span></p>\n<p>- <span><strong>Database Creator</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong><code>IAMFullAccess</code></strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. While this IAM policy provides broad permissions across AWS services, it does not specifically grant the necessary permissions to manage a data lake and create databases in AWS Lake Formation.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Lake Formation Data Analyst</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because this role just represents a user who can run queries against the data lake such as Amazon Athena. However, this role does not necessarily have permission to manage the data lake or create databases, which are required to resolve the “NotAuthorized” error in this scenario.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Lake Formation Data Catalog Full Access</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because this permission allows a user to access the data catalog in AWS Lake Formation. However, this permission does not include the ability to create databases or manage the data lake, which is required to resolve the “NotAuthorized” error in the given scenario.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/permissions-reference.html#persona-dl-admin\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/permissions-reference.html#persona-dl-admin</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/control-data-access-and-permissions-with-aws-lake-formation-and-amazon-emr/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/control-data-access-and-permissions-with-aws-lake-formation-and-amazon-emr/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Lake Formation Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":5,"sourceNumber":8,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company stores terabytes of data in an Amazon S3 bucket, which is accessed by multiple applications. The company uses a bucket policy to manage permissions. As application requirements evolve, the engineering team has to update the bucket policy accordingly. This process causes access issues to some applications due to misconfigurations.</p>\n<p>The company needs a way to control access for each application without disrupting the others.</p>\n<p>Which solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["Create individual IAM roles for each application and grant the necessary permissions to access the bucket.","Implement Amazon S3 Object Lock to manage permissions more securely.","Set up dedicated Amazon S3 Access Points for each application.","Utilize Amazon S3 Lifecycle policies to manage permissions based on the data lifecycle."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon S3 Access Points</strong> are a feature of Amazon S3 that simplifies managing data access for shared datasets. With Access Points, you can create multiple access points for a single S3 bucket, with each access point having a unique hostname and access policy tailored for a specific use case or application.</p>\n<p>Each access point can be configured with a custom access policy granting only the necessary permissions for its intended use.</p>\n<p>Access points allow separating access control for different applications rather than managing everything through a single bucket policy.</p>\n<p>Network controls can be applied to access points to restrict access to only requests from a specific VPC.</p>\n<p>Access points can be used to grant cross-account access while still retaining control over the bucket and data through the bucket policy.</p>\n<p>Aliases are automatically generated for each access point, making them interchangeable with bucket names in AWS APIs and CLI.</p>\n<p>Common S3 operations can be performed by replacing the bucket name with the access point ARN or alias.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/3b50e5c029d235f8ed3366ab7298d16da4dc37a9411a62210f5f989ebcc88815.png\"></p>\n<p>The company can meet its requirement of controlling access for each application without disrupting the others by setting up dedicated Amazon S3 Access Points for different applications to streamline access management.</p>\n<p>A custom policy attached to each Access Point will grant only the necessary permissions for that specific application.</p>\n<p>As application requirements change, only the policy of that application's Access Point needs updating, avoiding disruptions to other applications.</p>\n<p>This prevents issues caused by other applications from being affected when one application's permissions need to be updated. Developers no longer need to change the overall bucket policy frequently as their application ecosystem evolves.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <strong><span>Set up dedicated Amazon S3 Access Points for each application.</span></strong></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create individual IAM roles for each application and grant the necessary permissions to access the bucket</strong></span> is incorrect. This approach makes managing and auditing permissions more complicated and time-consuming because you need to grant correct permissions for each app's IAM role. It also increases security risks, as one might give applications access to more AWS services than they need.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Implement Amazon S3 Object Lock to manage permissions more securely</strong></span> is incorrect because S3 Object Lock is designed to prevent objects from being deleted or overwritten for a fixed amount of time or indefinitely, which is not directly related to simplifying permission management or preventing disruptions due to policy misconfigurations.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize Amazon S3 Lifecycle policies to manage permissions based on the data lifecycle</strong></span> is incorrect because S3 Lifecycle policies are used to manage objects' lifecycle, such as transitioning objects to less expensive storage classes or automatically deleting them after a certain period, and do not directly help with permission management or preventing configuration errors.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/access-points.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/access-points.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/access-points/\">https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/access-points/</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check this Amazon S3 Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":6,"sourceNumber":14,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineering team in a large organization is looking for a cost-effective solution to handle their non-urgent data integration workloads. These workloads include tasks such as testing and one-time data loads, which do not require fast job startup times or consistent runtimes. They are considering a solution that runs on spare compute capacity instead of dedicated hardware, which can lead to variable start and run times.</p>\n<p>Which of the following would be the most suitable for this scenario?</p>","answers":["AWS Glue Standard","AWS Glue Development Endpoint","AWS Glue Flex","AWS Glue Streaming ETL"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Glue Flex</strong> is designed for non-urgent data integration workloads that do not require fast job startup times or consistent runtimes. It runs on spare compute capacity instead of dedicated hardware, which can lead to variable start and runtimes but is more cost-effective.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/ecdca33bb35099139fb1ff7fadd11e891888c6d71427e56a47bfd3f8a30850f4.png\"></p>\n<p>With AWS Glue Flex, jobs run on spare compute capacity in AWS instead of dedicated hardware. This means that the start and runtimes of jobs using Flex can vary because spare compute resources aren’t always readily available and can be reclaimed during the run of a job. However, this approach is more cost-effective for non-urgent workloads.</p>\n<p>Regardless of the execution option used, AWS Glue jobs have the same capabilities, including access to custom connectors, visual authoring interface, job scheduling, and Glue Auto Scaling. The Flex execution class is available for AWS Glue 3.0 Spark jobs.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>AWS Glue Flex.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>AWS Glue Standard</strong></span> is incorrect because it is designed for time-sensitive tasks that require fast job startup times and consistent runtimes, achieved through the allocation of dedicated resources, making it more expensive and less flexible.</p>\n<p><strong><span>AWS Glue Development Endpoint</span></strong> is incorrect because it is specifically built for active development and testing of ETL scripts, not for running production workloads.</p>\n<p><strong><span>AWS Glue Streaming ETL</span></strong> is incorrect because this service is primarily used for streaming ETL jobs, which require real-time processing. It’s not designed for non-urgent workloads that can tolerate variable start and runtimes.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-flex-jobs-cost-savings-on-etl-workloads/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-flex-jobs-cost-savings-on-etl-workloads/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":7,"sourceNumber":16,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p><span>A multinational corporation is working on a large-scale data processing project. The project will continuously consume streaming data from an Apache Kafka cluster, transform the data in-flight, and deliver it into an Amazon S3 bucket. The tasks involved in this project are complex and long-running, often taking over 15 minutes to complete.</span></p>\n<p><span>Which AWS service would be the best choice to handle these long-running tasks? </span></p>","answers":["AWS Glue","AWS Lambda","Amazon Data Firehose","AWS Batch"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>AWS Glue is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy for users to prepare and load their data for analytics. It's designed to handle complex tasks that often take over 15 minutes to complete, which aligns with the requirements of the scenario.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/db5b79b838cd1a7d75258f897436ba1e65fbe4b3658af3c1a2122abb5d3ed823.png\" alt=\"AWS Glue\"></p>\n<p>You can create streaming extract, transform, and load (ETL) jobs that run continuously and consume data from streaming sources like Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, Apache Kafka, and Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (Amazon MSK). The jobs cleanse and transform the data and then load the results into Amazon S3 data lakes or JDBC data stores.</p>\n<p>In the scenario where a streaming ETL is required, AWS Glue is the best choice because it's designed for continuous streaming data processing. It can handle long tasks by transforming and loading data into Amazon S3 in real-time, which matches the project's needs for handling streaming data from Apache Kafka and delivering it efficiently to S3.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>AWS Glue.</strong></span></p>\n<p><strong><span>AWS Lambda</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect because it is a serverless compute service that runs your code in response to events and automatically manages the underlying compute resources for you. However, AWS Lambda functions have a maximum execution time of 15 minutes. This makes it unsuitable for tasks that often take over 15 minutes to complete, as specified in the scenario.</p>\n<p><span><strong>Amazon Data Firehose</strong></span> is incorrect because it is a fully managed service that delivers real-time streaming data to destinations such as Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, Amazon OpenSearch Service, and Splunk. While it typically supports data transformation through AWS Lambda, the complexity and duration of the tasks described in the scenario exceed the capabilities of simple transformations, which is the 15-minute execution limit of AWS Lambda functions.</p>\n<p><strong><span>AWS Batch</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect because it primarily enables developers, scientists, and engineers to easily and efficiently run hundreds of thousands of batch computing jobs on AWS.&nbsp;However, it cannot directly consume data from a streaming source. It is more suited for scenarios where workloads are not continuous streams but rather discrete batches of data that can be processed independently.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automate-alerting-and-reporting-for-aws-glue-job-resource-usage/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automate-alerting-and-reporting-for-aws-glue-job-resource-usage/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":8,"sourceNumber":17,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p><span>A data analyst is working with a large dataset stored in Amazon Redshift. The analyst needs to perform a complex data processing task that requires a custom function. The function will be used as part of a SQL query. The analyst wants to accomplish this with the least amount of effort and overhead.</span></p>\n<p><span>Which of the following options should the analyst choose? </span></p>","answers":["Create a User-Defined Function (UDF) directly in Redshift.","Integrate Amazon Athena with Redshift using a custom UDF via the Athena Query Federation SDK.","Use Amazon EMR to process the data and then import the results into Redshift.","Create a custom function using AWS Glue, register it in the Glue Data Catalog, and then access it from Redshift."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Amazon Redshift, a fully managed, petabyte-scale data warehouse service, allows you to create custom scalar UDFs using either a SQL SELECT clause or a Python program. Once created, the UDF is stored in the database and is available for any user with sufficient privileges to run.</p>\n<p>Running a custom scalar UDF in Amazon Redshift is as straightforward as running existing Amazon Redshift functions. This means you don’t need to learn new ways of invoking functions, saving you time and effort.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/c727355caa34901cd1f08deb960b6940e6dffe7d487556ede59a14975b6b76e6.png\" alt=\"Redshift User-Define Functions\"></p>\n<p>For Python UDFs, you can even import your own custom Python modules. This allows you to leverage pre-existing code and libraries, further enhancing the power and flexibility of your UDFs.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/82d7f87a030a2fbd83dd09765f332e6f19d552de935dd81ded89201ad2cd81f3.png\" alt=\"Redshift User-Defined Functions\"></p>\n<p>Moreover, UDFs in Redshift provide more control and flexibility over data processing workflows and potentially improve performance by reducing the amount of data transferred between the database and client application. This is because the computations are done within the database itself, rather than transferring data to an external location for processing.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Create a User-Defined Function (UDF) directly in Redshift.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Integrate Amazon Athena with Redshift using a custom UDF via the Athena Query Federation SDK</span> </strong>is incorrect. While it is possible to create custom UDFs in Amazon Athena using the Athena Query Federation SDK, integrating these UDFs with Amazon Redshift would require additional effort and overhead. You would need to write the UDF in Java, package it into a JAR file, deploy it using AWS Lambda, and then manage the integration between Amazon Athena and Amazon Redshift. This process primarily involves multiple steps and services, making it more complex and time-consuming compared to creating a UDF directly in Amazon Redshift.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use Amazon EMR to process the data and then import the results into Redshift </strong></span>is incorrect. Amazon EMR is just a cloud-native big data platform that allows you to process large amounts of data quickly and cost-effectively. However, using Amazon EMR to process the data and then importing the results into Amazon Redshift would require additional effort and overhead. You would need to set up an EMR cluster, write the processing code, run the job, and then import the results into Amazon Redshift.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Create a custom function using AWS Glue, register it in the Glue Data Catalog, and then access it from Redshift</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect. While AWS Glue allows you to create custom functions and register them in the AWS Glue Data Catalog, accessing these functions from Amazon Redshift would require additional steps and overhead. You would need to write the function code, register it in the AWS Glue Data Catalog using the&nbsp;<code>aws glue create-user-defined-function</code>&nbsp;command in the AWS CLI, and then configure Amazon Redshift to access the function.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/user-defined-functions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/user-defined-functions.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/user-defined-functions-for-amazon-redshift/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/user-defined-functions-for-amazon-redshift/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":9,"sourceNumber":18,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineer has developed an Extract, Load, Transform (ETL) process that loads data from an S3 bucket into an Amazon Redshift cluster. The current process uses the <code>COPY</code> command to ingest data to the Redshift cluster. The company wants to improve the efficiency of its current process.</p>\n<p>Which additional step should be implemented as part of the new ETL process?</p>","answers":["Load the data to an external table using Amazon Redshift Spectrum.","Create a temporary staging table to hold the data for transformation.","<span>Ingest data in bulk using the <code>UNLOAD</code> command.</span>","<span>Load data into the Amazon Redshift cluster with the <code>S3DistCp</code> command.</span>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>A <strong>COPY</strong> command is the most efficient way to load a table. You can also add data to your tables using INSERT commands, though it is much less efficient than using COPY. The COPY command is able to read from multiple data files or multiple data streams simultaneously. Amazon Redshift allocates the workload to the cluster nodes and performs the load operations in parallel, including sorting the rows and distributing data across node slices.</p>\n<p>You can use temporary staging tables to hold the data for transformation. These tables are automatically dropped after the ETL session is complete. Temporary tables can be created using the <code>CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE</code> syntax, or by issuing a <code>SELECT … INTO #TEMP_TABLE</code> query. Explicitly specifying the <code>CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE</code> statement allows you to control the DISTRIBUTION KEY, SORT KEY, and compression settings to further improve performance.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/43831a2b389e436182fbf98a90fdd4e37b30060bc95c3c9d65e43f4e281a8635.png\"></p>\n<p>A temporary table acts like a normal table but has a lifetime of a single SQL session. The proper use of temporary tables can significantly improve the performance of some ETL operations. Unlike regular permanent tables, data changes made to temporary tables don’t trigger automatic incremental backups to Amazon S3, and they don’t require synchronous block mirroring to store a redundant copy of data on a different compute node. Due to these reasons, data ingestion on temporary tables involves reduced overhead and performs much faster.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Create a temporary staging table to hold the data for transformation. </strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Load the data to an external table using Amazon Redshift Spectrum&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect. Redshift Spectrum just offers a way for querying data directly on Amazon S3. It won't help improve the efficiency of loading data into Redshift.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Ingest data in bulk using the <code>UNLOAD</code> command&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect because the UNLOAD command literally 'unloads' or moves the result of your Redshift query to Amazon S3 and not the other way around.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Load data into the Amazon Redshift cluster with the <code>S3DistCp</code> command&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect because the S3DistCp tool is primarily used in Amazon EMR, not in Redshift, to load data from Amazon S3 to HDFS.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Loading-data-from-S3.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Loading-data-from-S3.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-8-best-practices-for-high-performance-etl-processing-using-amazon-redshift/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-8-best-practices-for-high-performance-etl-processing-using-amazon-redshift/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_COPY.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_COPY.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":10,"sourceNumber":19,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineering team has recently implemented an ETL workload that leverages various AWS services. Given the complexity and scale of their operations, the team now seeks a solution that will enable them to generate custom reports of their costs and usage across these services. The goal is to analyze spending habits and find areas to cut costs.</p>\n<p>Which solution will meet this requirement?</p>","answers":["Implement AWS Budgets to set custom cost and usage budgets that alert when thresholds are exceeded.","Utilize AWS Cost Explorer to create and retrieve customizable reports that analyze AWS costs and usage data.","Deploy Amazon QuickSight for visualizing and analyzing AWS costs and usage data through dashboards.","Configure Amazon CloudWatch to monitor and alert on AWS service usage and associated costs."],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Cost Explorer</strong> is a tool designed to provide a comprehensive view of your AWS costs and usage over time, helping you better manage your AWS resources. It provides the following benefits:</p>\n<p><strong>Custom reporting</strong> - You can create custom reports to analyze your cost and usage data at different levels of granularity, like total costs across all accounts or specific costs by service, region, etc. This allows you to gain insights into your spending patterns.</p>\n<p><strong>Cost optimization</strong> - Cost Explorer provides visibility into your usage at different levels, which helps you identify opportunities to optimize costs, like reducing the usage of underutilized resources.</p>\n<p><strong>Trend analysis</strong> - With Cost Explorer, you can analyze cost and usage trends over multiple years at monthly granularity. This helps understand fluctuations and plan budgets accordingly.</p>\n<p><strong>Resource level data</strong> - It provides cost attribution down to the individual resource level, like EC2 instances, allowing you to pinpoint expensive resources consuming the most costs.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/5eef491e8d08815e8472d4cd75145848954c78fb54872ac425f7c425809c27fe.jpg\"></p>\n<p>Cost Explorer would be a good fit for the data engineering team as it would allow them to generate customized reports on costs and usage of various AWS services used in their ETL workload. This will help them gain insights into spending patterns and identify opportunities to optimize costs, like rightsizing resources or reducing the usage of underutilized ones.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Utilize AWS Cost Explorer to create and retrieve customizable reports that analyze AWS costs and usage data.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Implement AWS Budgets to set custom cost and usage budgets that alert when thresholds are exceeded</strong></span> is incorrect because AWS Budgets primarily focuses on monitoring and controlling AWS costs by setting budgets and alerts rather than generating detailed reports on costs and usage patterns.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Deploy Amazon QuickSight for visualizing and analyzing AWS costs and usage data through dashboards</strong></span> is incorrect because, although Amazon QuickSight can be used for visualization and analysis, it would require the data to be imported and processed, making it a more complex solution compared to directly using AWS Cost Explorer for cost analysis.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure Amazon CloudWatch to monitor and alert on AWS service usage and associated costs</strong></span> is incorrect because Amazon CloudWatch is primarily a monitoring service for AWS cloud resources and applications, not tailored for detailed cost and usage reporting.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cost-management/latest/userguide/ce-what-is.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cost-management/latest/userguide/ce-what-is.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws-cloud-financial-management/extended-history-and-more-granular-data-available-within-aws-cost-explorer/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws-cloud-financial-management/extended-history-and-more-granular-data-available-within-aws-cost-explorer/</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Billing and Cost Management Cheat Sheet:</strong><br>\n<a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-billing-and-cost-management/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-billing-and-cost-management/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":11,"sourceNumber":20,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineer is designing an ETL workflow that would export 5 TB of data to Amazon S3 every day. The data is sourced from a variety of platforms, including Oracle DB, Cassandra, Amazon Aurora, and Apache Kafka, with the schemas being frequently updated.</p>\n<p>To address this, the data engineer needs a robust solution capable of schema detection for these heterogeneous data sources.</p>\n<p>Which solution has the LEAST operational overhead for this requirement?</p>","answers":["Configure AWS Data Pipeline to orchestrate data movement and processing tasks, leveraging PySpark in AWS Lambda for ETL operations.","Utilize AWS Glue crawlers for schema detection and AWS Glue Spark jobs for data transformation.","Create an Amazon Redshift cluster as a staging area. Use a stored procedure for schema identification and data transformation.","Develop a solution that incorporates Amazon Athena for schema detection, executing ETL tasks through SQL queries against S3 data."],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Glue</strong> is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy for customers to prepare and load data for analytics. It provides capabilities for schema detection, ETL jobs, and crawlers that make it useful for working with dynamic or changing schemas.</p>\n<p><strong>AWS Glue crawlers</strong> automatically detect schemas for data sources. Glue crawlers scan data sources, detect schemas, and store the associated metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. This enables Glue to handle changing schemas without requiring code changes. The crawlers run periodically to detect new or changed data.</p>\n<p><strong>AWS Glue jobs</strong> can then be used to process the data. Glue jobs are defined using Python or Scala scripts that reference the schemas and metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. This allows Glue jobs to work with changing schemas without needing redeploys as the underlying data changes.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/e9fd631757956c26ca9462f60441fbfaaa134b67786ddbd2493d33fc98b4473f.jpg\"></p>\n<p>Given the requirements for schema detection, ETL capabilities, and the need to handle dynamic schemas, AWS Glue stands out as the most suitable option. It offers a managed environment that significantly reduces the operational overhead of managing infrastructure for ETL processes. Glue Crawlers automatically detect and adapt to schema changes, ensuring that the ETL process remains robust against evolving data structures. Glue Jobs then allow for the transformation and loading of data into Amazon S3 within the required timeframe.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Utilize AWS Glue crawlers for schema detection and AWS Glue Spark jobs for data transformation.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure AWS Data Pipeline to orchestrate data movement and processing tasks, leveraging PySpark in AWS Lambda for ETL operations</strong></span> is incorrect. AWS Data Pipeline is a web service for orchestrating data movements and processing tasks. While it can integrate with AWS Lambda for custom processing logic, using PySpark within Lambda poses limitations on execution time and resource allocation, which may not be suitable for processing 5 TB of data daily.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create an Amazon Redshift cluster as a staging area. Use a stored procedure for schema identification and data transformation</strong></span> is incorrect. Using Amazon Redshift as a staging area would require significant engineering effort due to the necessity of adapting disparate data formats, schemas, and types to Redshift's structured, columnar storage system. Moreover, a stored procedure is just a set of SQL statements saved together as a single executable entity. While it can execute complex logic, it's not inherently designed for dynamic schema detection across diverse data sources.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Develop a solution that incorporates Amazon Athena for schema detection, executing ETL tasks through SQL queries against S3 data</strong></span> is incorrect. Athena is primarily a query service that does not inherently provide ETL capabilities or schema detection in the manner required for this scenario. It is designed for querying data directly in S3 using SQL, not for the ETL process itself.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong><br>\n<a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":12,"sourceNumber":21,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p><span>A data engineering team has developed a stored procedure in Amazon Redshift to streamline data processing tasks. The procedure benefits from the Redshift query optimizer to ensure efficient execution.</span></p>\n<p><span>A data analyst, already a user of the Redshift database, needs permission to run this stored procedure for data analysis purposes.</span></p>\n<p><span>How can the team grant this access while adhering to the principle of least privilege?</span></p>","answers":["<span>Grant the data analyst the <code>REFERENCES</code> privilege on the procedure.</span>","<span>Grant the data analyst the <code>USAGE</code> privilege on the schema containing the procedure.</span>","Grant the data analyst the <code>EXECUTE</code> privilege on the procedure.","Grant the data analyst the <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code> privileges on the table used by the procedure."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>A <strong>stored procedure in Amazon Redshift</strong> is a piece of SQL code that has been pre-written and can be re-executed multiple times. Stored procedures allow encapsulating logic for data transformation, validation, and business rules.</p>\n<p>Here are some permissions related to stored procedures in Amazon Redshift:</p>\n<p><strong>EXECUTE</strong> permission allows users to run a stored procedure. This is the main permission required to allow users to execute procedures. <code>GRANT EXECUTE</code> is used to provide this permission.</p>\n<p><strong>INSERT, UPDATE, </strong>and<strong> DELETE</strong> permissions on underlying tables or views allow read/write access.</p>\n<p><strong>USAGE</strong> permission on schemas is required to allow creating and managing procedures within that schema.</p>\n<p><strong>ALTER</strong> permission on the procedure allows modifying the procedure definition after creation.</p>\n<p><strong>REFERENCES</strong> permission allows using the procedure name in SQL statements like CALL.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/6906281dae9bbd281f373cb42b3b97284e9e097ca8fd59b53d674f56f1cbf8af.jpg\" alt=\"Stored procedure in Amazon Redshift\"></p>\n<p>To provide the data analyst permission to call the stored procedure, the data engineering team can create a role in Amazon Redshift and grant EXECUTE permission on a stored procedure to the role using the <code>GRANT</code> statement. Add the data analyst to the role to allow them to execute the stored procedure and perform data analysis tasks while restricting access to the procedure only. This gives the data engineering team control over database permissions and access.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <strong><span>Grant the data analyst the <code>EXECUTE</code> privilege on the procedure.</span></strong></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Grant the data analyst the <code>REFERENCES</code> privilege on the procedure</strong></span> is incorrect. The <code>REFERENCES</code> privilege is primarily used in the context of creating a foreign key constraint and does not apply to the execution of stored procedures. It would not enable the data analyst to execute the stored procedure.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Grant the data analyst the <code>USAGE</code> privilege on the schema containing the procedure</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. While <code>USAGE</code> on a schema grants the ability to access objects within that schema, it does not specifically allow for the execution of stored procedures. <code>USAGE</code> might be necessary in addition to <code>EXECUTE</code> for certain scenarios, but on its own, it does not grant permission to execute stored procedures.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Grant the data analyst the <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code> privileges on the table used by the procedure</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. These privileges just allow users to perform table data manipulation operations, such as adding, modifying, or removing data. However, they do not provide permission to execute stored procedures. These privileges are unrelated to the execution of stored procedures and would not enable the data analyst to run the stored procedure.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/stored-procedure-security-and-privileges.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/stored-procedure-security-and-privileges.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_GRANT.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_GRANT.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":13,"sourceNumber":22,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p><span>A data engineering team is tasked with migrating and continuously replicating data from an on-premises Microsoft SQL Server database to Amazon <span><span><span>RDS</span></span></span> for SQL Server. The team intends to use AWS Database Migration Service (AWS <span><span><span>DMS</span></span></span>) with a <span>DMS</span> replication instance for both initial migration and subsequent data change synchronization. The objective is to implement a solution that requires minimal manual effort.</span></p>\n<p><span>Which combination of actions should the team take to fulfill these requirements? (Select TWO.)</span></p>","answers":["Turn on the full transaction log settings on the on-premises SQL Server.","<span>Create a DMS task in Full load plus <code>CHANGE DATA CAPTURE (CDC)</code> mode.</span>","Create an AWS Datasync task to run one-time transfers for migration of the on-premises SQL Server.","Use the AWS Schema Conversion Tool (SCT) to convert the source database schema to be compatible with the target database.","<span>Create a DMS task in <code>CHANGE DATA CAPTURE (CDC) only</code> mode.</span>"],"correct":[0,1],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)</strong> allows you to migrate databases to AWS easily and securely. It supports migrations between on-premises and AWS databases and between different AWS database services. The source or target of the migration must be on AWS.</p>\n<p>An AWS DMS task can be created to capture ongoing changes from the source data store during the data migration process. Additionally, a task can be configured to capture ongoing changes after completing the initial full-load migration to a supported target data store. This process, known as ongoing replication or change data capture (CDC), is employed by AWS DMS when replicating ongoing changes from a source data store. The CDC process works by collecting changes to the database logs using the database engine's native API.</p>\n<p>There are two types of ongoing replication tasks available in AWS DMS:</p>\n<p><strong>Full load plus Change data capture (CDC)</strong> – The task migrates existing data and then updates the target database based on changes to the source database.</p>\n<p><strong>Change data capture (CDC) only</strong> – The task migrates ongoing changes after you have data on your target database.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/aacbd939d2845b3486f2fb943c48ce52bb78d2135209f27a167c3ef59ca12287.png\" alt=\"AWS DMS\"></p>\n<p>There are <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Source.SQLServer.html\">different prerequisites</a> that must be configured in the source database to allow Change Data Capture (CDC) to take place. Most commonly, transaction logs need to be enabled, which depends on the specific database being used. For example, with Microsoft SQL Server as the source, full transaction logging must be turned on to enable CDC. On the other hand, if the source is a MySQL database, binary logging must be enabled first before CDC can be utilized.</p>\n<p>In the scenario, since the requirement is to conduct an initial migration from the on-premises Microsoft SQL Server database to Amazon RDS for SQL Server, followed by capturing ongoing changes thereafter, an AWS DMS task that runs in the Full load plus CDC (Change Data Capture) mode must be used. This mode ensures that the initial full load migration is performed, and subsequent data changes are continuously replicated to the target database.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span><strong>- Turn on the full transaction log settings on the on-premises SQL Server.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>- Create a DMS task in Full load plus <code>CHANGE DATA CAPTURE (CDC)</code> mode.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create an AWS Datasync task to run one-time transfers for migration of the on-premises SQL Server</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. AWS DataSync is primarily used for transferring files and objects between on-premises storage and AWS storage services, not for migrating databases. Also, one-time transfers would not fulfill the requirement for subsequent data change synchronization after the initial migration.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use the AWS Schema Conversion Tool (SCT) to convert the source database schema to be compatible with the target database</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. AWS SCT is not required in this case since the source and target databases are both SQL Server instances, and no schema conversion is necessary.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create a DMS task in <code>CHANGE DATA CAPTURE (CDC) only</code> mode</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. This option can only work if the initial full load migration has already been completed by another method. However, the scenario requires a solution for both initial migration and subsequent data change synchronization.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-on-premises-microsoft-sql-server-database-to-amazon-rds-for-sql-server.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-on-premises-microsoft-sql-server-database-to-amazon-rds-for-sql-server.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Source.SQLServer.html#CHAP_Source.SQLServer.Prerequisites\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Source.SQLServer.html#CHAP_Source.SQLServer.Prerequisites</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS DMS Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-database-migration-service/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-database-migration-service/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":14,"sourceNumber":24,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineering team is automating the process to extract, transform, and load (ETL) gzip-compressed JSON files from an Amazon S3 bucket. The gzip files are uploaded daily, with each having a total size of less than 30 MB. As part of the transformation logic, the team needs to remove duplicate records using <code>DataFrame.drop_duplicates()</code> before loading the processed data. The goal is to implement the ETL pipeline in the most cost-effective way.</p>\n<p>Which action should be taken to meet this requirement?</p>","answers":["Use AWS Glue for Ray to write Python scripts for data transformation.","Utilize AWS Glue on Apache Spark, using PySpark for data transformation.","Use an AWS Glue job using Python Shell and pandas for data transformation.","Utilize AWS Glue on Apache Spark, writing the logic in Scala for data transformation."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Glue</strong> is a serverless data integration service that makes it easier to discover, prepare, and combine data for analytics, machine learning (ML), and application development. AWS Glue provides all the capabilities needed for data integration, so you can start analyzing your data and putting it to use in minutes instead of months. AWS Glue provides both visual and code-based interfaces to make data integration easier. Users can more easily find and access data using the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Data engineers and ETL (extract, transform, and load) developers can visually create, run, and monitor ETL workflows.</p>\n<p>AWS Glue offers different engines on which you can run jobs, providing flexibility for various data processing needs:</p>\n<p><strong>Spark:</strong> AWS Glue on Apache Spark is designed for complex ETL jobs that require processing large volumes of data. Spark's distributed computing model enables high-performance data transformation and analysis across clusters of computers. It's ideal for tasks that need to handle big data sets, perform extensive data manipulation, or require the use of Spark's rich ecosystem of libraries.</p>\n<p><strong>Ray:</strong> AWS Glue for Ray is optimized for highly parallel and compute-intensive tasks. Ray is a framework that allows for easy scaling of applications from a laptop to a cluster, making it suitable for machine learning workflows, real-time data processing, and tasks that require concurrency.</p>\n<p><strong>Python Shell:</strong> AWS Glue's Python Shell is best suited for light to medium data transformation tasks that do not necessitate the distributed computing capabilities of Spark or Ray. It allows for simpler, script-based ETL jobs that can be executed without the overhead of setting up a Spark or Ray environment.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/bf2df14672760afc4537ab798effc0fe0cd199fb5d4709dad2e30e1ca6885c1f.png\" alt=\"AWS Glue Studio console Selecting the script engine (Python shell, Ray, or Spark)\"></p>\n<p>With AWS Glue, you only pay for the time that your ETL job takes to run. There are no resources to manage, no upfront costs, and you are not charged for startup or shutdown time. AWS charges an hourly rate based on the number of data processing units (DPUs) used to run your ETL job. A single standard DPU provides 4 vCPU and 16 GB of memory, whereas a high-memory DPU (M-DPU) provides 4 vCPU and 32 GB of memory.</p>\n<p>In simple ETL tasks, such as the given scenario of processing small gzip files, using AWS Glue's Python Shell proves highly cost-effective. The data engineering team can opt to allocate only 1/16th of a DPU for the ETL job. This approach allows them to execute their tasks economically, paying only for the minimal computing power required and avoiding the higher expenses associated with more resource-intensive options like Spark or Ray.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Use an AWS Glue job using Python Shell and pandas for data transformation.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use AWS Glue for Ray to write Python scripts for data transformation</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because Ray is primarily used for large-scale parallel data processing, which might be overkill for processing files under 30 MB. Moreover, jobs that use AWS Glue for Ray are more expensive to run than those using Python Shell.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize AWS Glue on Apache Spark, using PySpark for data transformation</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. AWS Glue with Apache Spark using PySpark is simply a powerful option, but it's not the most cost-effective for small files under 30MB. Spark is more suited for handling large-scale data processing, and the overhead it introduces might not be necessary for smaller files.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize AWS Glue on Apache Spark, writing the logic in Scala for data transformation</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Similar to the other incorrect option,&nbsp;this solution runs on the Spark engine, which might be more than necessary for small file processing and can increase costs.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-glue-best-practices-build-performant-data-pipeline/additional-considerations.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-glue-best-practices-build-performant-data-pipeline/additional-considerations.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/aws-glue-python-shell-now-supports-python-3-9-with-a-flexible-pre-loaded-environment-and-support-to-install-additional-libraries/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/aws-glue-python-shell-now-supports-python-3-9-with-a-flexible-pre-loaded-environment-and-support-to-install-additional-libraries/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":15,"sourceNumber":25,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineering team has a task to configure an ETL job using AWS Glue <code>DynamicFrame</code> to manage data in a partitioned Glue table. The table is optimized with partition indexes to enhance query efficiency. The team aims to refine the ETL operation by selectively processing necessary data within a <code>DynamicFrame</code>, using partition filtering and partition indexes to boost query performance and minimize costs.</p>\n<p>Which of the following is the MOST cost-effective solution that fulfills these requirements?</p>","answers":["<span>Utilize pushdown predicates to filter partitions upfront. Minimize data load by applying filters to the partition metadata within <code>DynamicFrame</code>.<span>\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</span></span>","<span>Transform the <code>DynamicFrame</code> into a Spark SQL DataFrame for efficient partitioned writing into Amazon S3.</span>","&lt;span&gt;Optimize the process using server-side filtering with the catalog partition predicates during &lt;code&gt;DynamicFrame&lt;/code&gt; creation.&lt;/span&gt;","Aggregate all input files from the source table into a unified in-memory partition before executing queries to simplify the data processing workflow."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Glue</strong>&nbsp;is a powerful ETL service that easily moves data between different data stores. By using AWS Glue, you can easily create and manage ETL jobs to transfer data from various sources, such as Amazon S3, Amazon RDS, and Amazon Redshift. Additionally, AWS Glue enables you to transform your data as needed to fit your specific needs. One of the key advantages of AWS Glue is its automatic schema discovery and mapping, which allows you to map data from different sources with different schemas easily.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/18e0c0355167f7d0fee8cc999690dcb52088e50656c1865da6ea1fa31c8f3d54.png\" alt=\"DynamicFrame\"></p>\n<p>AWS Glue's support for server-side filtering with catalog partition predicates directly during the creation of <code>DynamicFrame</code> is a powerful feature for optimizing ETL processes. This capability allows the ETL job to selectively process only the necessary data by utilizing the metadata catalog's partition indexes. Doing so significantly reduces the volume of data that needs to be read and processed, leading to reductions in execution time and cost.</p>\n<p>Server-side filtering applies filter predicates against the partition metadata stored in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. This means that before the data is even loaded into the <code>DynamicFrame</code>, AWS Glue can narrow down the data to just the relevant partitions based on the criteria specified by the ETL job. This approach is more efficient than client-side filtering, where data is first loaded into memory before being filtered, and it leverages the AWS Glue catalog's capabilities to minimize data scan and processing.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Optimize the process using server-side filtering with the catalog partition predicates during <code>DynamicFrame</code> creation.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize pushdown predicates to filter partitions upfront. Minimize data load by applying filters to the partition metadata within <code>DynamicFrame</code></strong></span> is incorrect.&nbsp;Pushdown predicates are less efficient than catalog partition predicates when used directly during <code>DynamicFrame</code> creation. This is because pushdown predicates apply filters after the <code>DynamicFrame</code> has been created, leading to higher initial data loads before the filter is applied.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Transform the <code>DynamicFrame</code> into a Spark SQL DataFrame for efficient partitioned writing into Amazon S3</strong></span> is incorrect&nbsp;because this approach simply focuses on the writing process rather than optimizing the reading process by filtering unnecessary data early on.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Aggregate all input files from the source table into a unified in-memory partition before executing queries to simplify the data processing workflow</strong>&nbsp;</span>is incorrect.&nbsp;This approach would typically increase costs and processing time due to the large volume of data being loaded into memory, contrary to the goal of minimizing costs.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-partitions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-partitions.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-glue-best-practices-build-performant-data-pipeline/building-a-performance-efficient-data-pipeline.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-glue-best-practices-build-performant-data-pipeline/building-a-performance-efficient-data-pipeline.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":16,"sourceNumber":26,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company runs an on-premises data processing system that uses an Apache Hadoop cluster to execute various batch processing jobs written in several open-source frameworks. These frameworks include Apache Spark, Apache MXNet, Apache HBase, Apache Hudi, Presto, et cetera. Due to an anticipated surge in data storage needs, the corporation is considering migrating to AWS to leverage enhanced storage scalability and durability while continuing to utilize its existing job scripts.</p>\n<p>Which solution will effectively fulfill these criteria at the LOWEST cost?</p>","answers":["Migrate to Amazon EMR and centralize data storage on Amazon S3. Utilize persistent EMR clusters for job execution.","Transition to Amazon EMR with a complete data migration to Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS). Expand the cluster with additional core nodes as storage demands increase.","Move into Amazon EMR. Configure to store the active data within HDFS and archival data in Amazon S3. Utilize Apache Hive for table integration across S3 and HDFS.","Transition the on-premises Apache Hadoop cluster to Amazon EMR. Centralize the data storage on Amazon S3 and initiate transient EMR clusters for task execution."],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon EMR</strong> is a managed service that allows you to process big data workloads using popular frameworks such as Apache Hadoop, Spark, HBase, and others. By migrating your on-premises Apache Hadoop cluster to Amazon EMR and storing your data centrally on Amazon S3, you can take advantage of AWS services' scalability, durability, and cost-effectiveness. Amazon S3 is a highly durable and scalable storage service that provides a cost-effective solution for storing large amounts of data. Utilizing Amazon EMR allows you to execute existing job scripts with minimal modifications while leveraging the managed scaling and cluster management features. Additionally, Amazon EMR supports transient clusters that can be created on demand for specific jobs and terminated once the job is completed. This feature can offer significant cost savings over persistent clusters that run continuously.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/7fa7d39e80fb936ae52fcd75d0989987dd0bdb75b81055012c4a400611de52e1.png\"></p>\n<p>Initiating transient EMR clusters for task execution aligns to minimize costs while meeting the surge in data storage and processing requirements. Transient clusters are created for the duration of specific tasks and terminated afterward, eliminating the cost of running idle resources. This approach, combined with storing data in Amazon S3, takes full advantage of AWS's pay-as-you-go pricing model, ensuring that storage is scalable and cost-efficient.</p>\n<p>Centralizing data in Amazon S3 also simplifies data management and enhances data durability and availability. This solution meets the company's immediate needs for scalability and durability. It does so in the most cost-effective manner possible, leveraging the best of what AWS offers for big data processing.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Transition the on-premises Apache Hadoop cluster to Amazon EMR. Centralize the data storage on Amazon S3 and initiate transient EMR clusters for task execution.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Migrate to Amazon EMR and centralize data storage on Amazon S3. Utilize persistent EMR clusters for job execution</strong></span> is incorrect.&nbsp;Using persistent EMR clusters for job execution is more expensive than using transient clusters that are terminated after the jobs are completed.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Transition to Amazon EMR with a complete data migration to Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS). Expand the cluster with additional core nodes as storage demands increase</strong></span> is incorrect. This approach involves higher costs due to the need for additional core nodes to expand the cluster, and it doesn't utilize the cost-effectiveness and scalability of Amazon S3 for storage.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Move into Amazon EMR. Configure to store the active data within HDFS and archival data in Amazon S3. Utilize Apache Hive for table integration across S3 and HDFS</strong></span> is incorrect. Although Amazon S3 is used for archival storage, Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS) requires EMR cluster resources to maintain active data; thus, it leads to higher costs compared to a solution that fully utilizes Amazon S3 for storage.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-instances-guidelines.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-instances-guidelines.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/emr/\">https://aws.amazon.com/emr/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/aws/aws-emr-best-practices/blob/main/content/reliability/best_practices.md\">https://github.com/aws/aws-emr-best-practices/blob/main/content/reliability/best_practices.md</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon EMR Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":17,"sourceNumber":27,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineering team configures an AWS Glue crawler in the <code>Tutorial's</code> AWS account to catalog VPC flow logs stored in an Amazon S3 bucket within the <code>Dojo</code>'s account. The goal is to ensure the AWS Glue crawler in the <code>Tutorial</code>'s account can access and catalog data seamlessly, focusing on integrating AWS services for cross-account resource access.</p>\n<p>Which combination of actions should be taken to fulfill these requirements? (Select THREE.)</p>","answers":["<span>Update the AWS Glue crawler's configuration in the <code>Tutorial</code>'s account to target the S3 bucket containing the VPC flow logs in the <code>Dojo</code>'s account.</span>","<span>Implement a bucket policy on the S3 bucket in the <code>Dojo</code>'s account that explicitly permits the AWS Glue crawler's IAM role in the <code>Tutorial</code>'s account access to the bucket.</span>","<span>Utilize AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM) in the <code>Dojo</code>'s account to share the S3 bucket with the <code>Tutorial</code>'s account, facilitating cross-account access.</span>","<span>Configure an IAM role in the <code>Tutorial</code>’s account with permissions to access the S3 bucket in the <code>Dojo</code>’s account and associate this role with the AWS Glue crawler.</span>","<span>Create a VPC Endpoint for S3 in <code>Dojo</code>'s account to streamline access between the AWS Glue crawler in the <code>Tutorial</code>'s account and the S3 bucket.</span>","<span>Establish a Direct Connect link between the <code>Tutorials</code> and <code>Dojo</code>'s accounts to ensure private connectivity for the AWS Glue crawler to access the S3 bucket.</span>"],"correct":[0,1,3],"explanation":"<p>AWS Glue is a completely managed service for ETL (extract, transform, load), simplifying the process for users to prepare and import their data for analytical purposes. The AWS Glue crawler can be configured to access data stores in different AWS accounts by using appropriate permissions and configurations. Cross-account access in AWS involves configuring resource-based policies and IAM roles to grant the necessary permissions across accounts.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/d277dd91559959aa7a73fc11fd8bdb72707e068d23a6fb2f4f2d620c80927d2d.png\"></p>\n<p>The administrator of Account A assigns a bucket policy that allows cross-account access, enabling Account B to execute certain operations on the bucket. It is important to recognize that the administrator of Account B gains these permissions by default. Subsequently, the administrator of Account B applies a user policy to an account user, passing on the rights obtained from Account A. Following this, the user in Account B tests the permissions by accessing a file in the bucket that is controlled by Account A.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span>-&nbsp;<strong>Update the AWS Glue crawler's configuration in the <code>Tutorial</code>'s account to target the S3 bucket containing the VPC flow logs in the <code>Dojo</code>'s account.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span>-&nbsp;<strong>Implement a bucket policy on the S3 bucket in the <code>Dojo</code>'s account that explicitly permits the AWS Glue crawler's IAM role in the <code>Tutorial</code>'s account access to the bucket.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span>-&nbsp;<strong>Configure an IAM role in the <code>Tutorial</code>’s account with permissions to access the S3 bucket in the <code>Dojo</code>’s account and associate this role with the AWS Glue crawler.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Utilize AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM) in the <code>Dojo</code>'s account to share the S3 bucket with the <code>Tutorial</code>'s account, facilitating cross-account access</strong></span> is incorrect&nbsp;because the AWS RAM service is primarily used for sharing AWS resources across accounts, but it does not directly facilitate access for AWS Glue crawlers to S3 buckets in another account.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Create a VPC Endpoint for S3 in <code>Dojo</code>'s account to streamline access between the AWS Glue crawler in the <code>Tutorial</code>'s account and the S3 bucket</span></strong>&nbsp;is incorrect&nbsp;because VPC Endpoints simply facilitate private connections between VPCs and AWS services within the same account and do not directly address cross-account access for AWS Glue crawlers.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Establish a Direct Connect link between the <code>Tutorials</code> and <code>Dojo</code>'s accounts to ensure private connectivity for the AWS Glue crawler to access the S3 bucket</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because, in this scenario, Direct Connect is only used for establishing a dedicated network connection from your on-premises data center to AWS and does not directly facilitate cross-account access for AWS Glue crawlers to S3 buckets.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-crawler.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-crawler.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/example-walkthroughs-managing-access-example2.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/example-walkthroughs-managing-access-example2.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":18,"sourceNumber":28,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company has expanded its AWS infrastructure, utilizing Amazon S3 for data storage and Amazon Athena for query execution. With growth, the complexity and volume of data have increased, leading to the adoption of Amazon EMR for advanced data processing using Apache Spark and machine learning libraries.</p>\n<p>The data collection is cataloged on both a Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS) via an Apache Hive metastore and a custom AWS Glue Data Catalog. The data engineering team faces the challenge of designing a durable data store that utilizes both storage systems.</p>\n<p>To achieve a centralized data management strategy, which approach should the data engineering team adopt with the LEAST migration effort?</p>","answers":["Migrate all data from Apache Hive to an Amazon S3 bucket to create a centralized data source data and utilize AWS Glue Data Catalog for metadata storage. Leverage Amazon EMR and Athena for data processing.","Consolidate all data in HDFS by moving the data from Amazon S3. Transition Athena queries to Presto on Amazon EMR. Continue utilizing Amazon EMR for data processing.","Centralize all data in Amazon S3. Configure Amazon EMR to integrate with AWS Glue Data Catalog and adopt Spark SQL for Athena queries. Maintain the use of Amazon EMR for ongoing data operations.","Move all data stored in Amazon S3 to HDFS. Convert Athena queries to Spark SQL, and use EMR File System (EMRFS) for data processing tasks."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>The AWS Glue Data Catalog serves as a centralized metadata repository, containing references to data employed as both origins and destinations for extract, transform, and load (ETL) tasks within AWS Glue. This catalog facilitates more efficient management of data sources, further enhanced by its compatibility with Amazon EMR, Amazon Athena, and other AWS services. Leveraging Amazon EMR, which supports various processing frameworks such as Apache Spark, expands capabilities for advanced data processing tasks, including batch processing, streaming, and machine learning.</p>\n<p>A database in the AWS Glue Data Catalog is a container that holds tables. You use databases to organize your tables into separate categories. Databases are created when you run a crawler or add a table manually. The database list in the AWS Glue console displays descriptions for all your databases.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/807e46d5bf9e6d8e71ce7ba2d51e402ead54403ee39356869e338960fb691349.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>The latest versions of Amazon EMR&nbsp;allow you to configure Apache Hive to use the AWS Glue Data Catalog as its metastore. This configuration is recommended when you require a persistent metastore or a metastore shared by different clusters, services, applications, or AWS accounts.</p>\n<p>Companies might want to create AWS Glue Data Catalog tables manually and then keep them updated with AWS Glue crawlers. Crawlers running on a schedule can add new partitions and update the tables with any schema changes. This also applies to tables migrated from an Apache Hive metastore.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Migrate all data from Apache Hive to an Amazon S3 bucket to create a centralized data source data and utilize AWS Glue Data Catalog for metadata storage. Leverage Amazon EMR and Athena for data processing.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Consolidate all data in HDFS by moving the data from Amazon S3. Transition Athena queries to Presto on Amazon EMR. Continue utilizing Amazon EMR for data processing&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect&nbsp;because it simply overlooks the primary concern of data durability. HDFS, when used within EMR, is considered an ephemeral storage that could potentially lead to data loss in the event of cluster termination.&nbsp;Additionally, this approach entails significant migration effort to shift from Athena to Presto, increasing complexity without addressing the need for a centralized data catalog.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Centralize all data in Amazon S3. Configure Amazon EMR to integrate with AWS Glue Data Catalog and adopt Spark SQL for Athena queries. Maintain the use of Amazon EMR for ongoing data operations&nbsp;</span></strong>is incorrect&nbsp;because it mainly suggests&nbsp;migrating Athena queries to Spark SQL. While integrating Amazon S3 with AWS Glue Data Catalog and Amazon EMR is beneficial, this move is not necessary as Athena can directly support SQL queries on data stored in S3 without the need for conversion to Spark SQL, making this step unnecessary and potentially adding complexity.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Move all data stored in Amazon S3 to HDFS. Convert Athena queries to Spark SQL, and use EMR File System (EMRFS) for data processing tasks&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect because it primarily relies on HDFS for centralized data storage, which does not offer the same level of durability and scalability as Amazon S3. This option also requires converting Athena queries to Spark SQL, which adds unnecessary migration effort and complexity without providing a clear benefit over using Athena for direct queries on data stored in Amazon S3. Moreover,&nbsp;EMRFS simply provides the convenience of storing persistent data in Amazon S3 for Amazon EMR and not for running data processing tasks.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-hive-metastore-glue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-hive-metastore-glue.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon S3 Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":19,"sourceNumber":29,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company is migrating data from its various on-premises file systems into an Amazon S3 data lake managed via the AWS Lake Formation service. To enhance analytics capabilities, the data engineer is tasked with creating a data ingestion pipeline that not only transfers data efficiently but also splits the data across multiple Amazon S3 buckets in near real-time to support different analytical workloads.</p>\n<p>Which of the following is the MOST operationally efficient solution that the data engineer should implement?</p>","answers":["Set up S3 Batch Operations for bulk data management and distribution across multiple S3 buckets.","Initially transfer the data using AWS Snowcone and implement S3 Replication Time Control (S3 RTC) to manage the replication of data across S3 buckets efficiently.","Utilize AWS DataSync for streamlined and fast data transfer to various S3 buckets.","Use Amazon Data Firehose for real-time data streaming and automatic distribution to multiple S3 buckets."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>AWS DataSync is specifically designed to accelerate the transfer of vast volumes of data from on-premises storage systems to AWS storage services such as Amazon S3. It supports fast, secure, and reliable data migration, making it an ideal choice for projects that require the distribution of data across multiple S3 buckets in near real-time. DataSync automates the replication process, optimizes the data transfer over the network, and seamlessly integrates with Amazon S3, allowing for the direct specification of multiple target buckets.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/cd86cda59cb064dbf20f07111170a6175c6310154764a4c47a4beb4ae7b7d86a.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>To transfer data to or from your Amazon S3 bucket, you need to create an AWS DataSync transfer location. DataSync uses this location as a source or destination for transferring data. Keep in mind that DataSync needs access to the S3 bucket that you're transferring to or from. To do this, you must create an AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) role that DataSync assumes with the permissions required to access the bucket. You can then specify this role when creating your Amazon S3 location for DataSync.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Utilize AWS DataSync for streamlined and fast data transfer to various S3 buckets.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Set up S3 Batch Operations for bulk data management and distribution across multiple S3 buckets&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect&nbsp;because it is primarily&nbsp;used for operations on existing S3 objects rather than the initial upload and real-time distribution of data. It does not meet the requirement for real-time data ingestion and distribution.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Initially transfer the data using AWS Snowcone and implement S3 Replication Time Control (S3 RTC) to manage the replication of data across S3 buckets efficiently&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect. Physically transferring data via an AWS Snowcone device is not necessary since the transfer can be fully done online. Moreover, the S3 RTC feature simply focuses on enhancing the replication speed and predictability of S3 objects within AWS, which does not directly address the need for initial data migration and near real-time distribution to multiple buckets.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use Amazon Data Firehose for real-time data streaming and automatic distribution to multiple S3 buckets&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect. Although the Amazon Data Firehose service is capable of handling real-time data streaming and can automatically load streaming data into Amazon S3, this service is primarily designed for streaming use cases rather than bulk data migrations from on-premises environments. Thus, this solution does not fully meet the requirements for this specific scenario.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datasync/latest/userguide/welcome.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datasync/latest/userguide/welcome.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datasync/latest/userguide/create-object-location.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datasync/latest/userguide/create-object-location.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS DataSync Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-datasync/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-datasync/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":20,"sourceNumber":30,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineering team at a healthcare startup is working on a project to analyze patient health records. They have a significant amount of patient health records stored in an external cloud data warehouse. The team’s goal is to migrate these large datasets to Amazon Redshift for advanced analytics.</p>\n<p>Which combination of steps would be the most efficient way to achieve this with MINIMAL effort? (Select TWO.)</p>","answers":["Utilize the AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT) to adapt the source schema and data definition language (DDL) script from the external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift.","Use the Amazon Athena notebook to adapt the source schema and data definition language (DDL) script from the external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift.","Use the Amazon Athena SQL query editor to transfer the data from the external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift.","<span>Export the data from the external cloud data warehouse into Amazon S3 as flat delimited text files. Then, use the Amazon Redshift SQL query editor with the <code>COPY</code> command to transfer the data from Amazon S3 to Amazon Redshift.</span>","Leverage AWS Glue Studio to transfer the data from the external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift."],"correct":[0,4],"explanation":"<p>AWS SCT is a tool that significantly reduces the effort to migrate to Amazon Redshift by automatically converting the source schema and a majority of the custom code to a format compatible with Amazon Redshift. The converted code can be in the form of views, stored procedures, and functions.</p>\n<p>In this scenario, the team needs to migrate patient health records from an external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift. The first step in this process is to ensure that the schema and DDL scripts of the external cloud data warehouse are compatible with Amazon Redshift. This is where AWS SCT comes in. By using AWS SCT, the team can automatically convert the source schema and DDL scripts to a format that Amazon Redshift can understand, thereby reducing the manual effort required in the migration process.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/4772ab5110b9cce2b7d202d0bc41dd23f9309483822cf0ef1de5e8aa80215d2d.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>AWS Glue Studio is a visual interface for AWS Glue that simplifies the process of creating, running, and monitoring ETL jobs. ETL stands for Extract, Transform, and Load, which are the three main steps in data integration processes.</p>\n<p>In the given scenario, the team needs to migrate patient health records from an external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift. After the schemas and DDL scripts have been converted using AWS SCT, the next step is to transfer the data itself. AWS Glue Studio can be used to create an ETL job that extracts the data from the external cloud data warehouse, transforms the data if necessary (for example, cleaning the data or converting data types), and then loads the transformed data into Amazon Redshift. This process is automated, which means the team can migrate large datasets with minimal effort.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span><strong>- Utilize the AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT) to adapt the source schema and data definition language (DDL) script from the external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>- Leverage AWS Glue Studio to transfer the data from the external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:<span><strong>&nbsp;Use the Amazon Athena notebook to adapt the source schema and data definition language (DDL) script from the external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift</strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect&nbsp;because Amazon Athena is simply an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. It is not designed to convert schemas or DDL scripts from one database to another.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use the Amazon Athena SQL query editor to transfer the data from the external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift</strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect. It is true that Amazon Athena can be used to query data directly in S3, but this service is not designed to transfer data from an external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift. Using AWS Glue Studio is a better option for easily transferring data to Redshift.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Export the data from the external cloud data warehouse into Amazon S3 as flat delimited text files. Then, use the Amazon Redshift SQL query editor with the <code>COPY</code> command to transfer the data from Amazon S3 to Amazon Redshift</strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect&nbsp;because while this method could technically work, it would require significant manual effort and is not the most efficient way to transfer data from an external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift. A more efficient way would be to use AWS Glue Studio or AWS SCT, which can automate the process and handle larger datasets more effectively.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/SchemaConversionTool/latest/userguide/CHAP_Welcome.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/SchemaConversionTool/latest/userguide/CHAP_Welcome.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/SchemaConversionTool/latest/userguide/CHAP_Converting.DW.html#CHAP_Converting.DW.Convert\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/SchemaConversionTool/latest/userguide/CHAP_Converting.DW.html#CHAP_Converting.DW.Convert</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":21,"sourceNumber":31,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A manufacturing company is in the process of establishing a cloud-based data analytics solution using an Amazon Redshift data warehouse. This new setup will empower the company to conduct an in-depth bi-weekly analysis of its production efficiency and inventory levels.</p>\n<p>Due to strict regulatory requirements, the company seeks an effective method to oversee and document all activities within its Amazon Redshift resource. The aim is to systematically record SQL queries, user actions, and alterations to databases and database objects for audit and traceability purposes.</p>\n<p>Which solution would satisfy these requirements with the LEAST amount of operational overhead involved?</p>","answers":["Parse Amazon CloudWatch Logs from the Redshift cluster using an AWS Lambda function. Store logs in Amazon OpenSearch Service for detailed analysis.","Integrate Amazon CloudWatch Logs with Amazon Redshift to collect Redshift SQL query logs. Utilize Amazon CloudWatch alarms and Amazon SNS to alert on any anomalies.","Enable Amazon Redshift's built-in audit logging feature to capture detailed database transactions, including SQL query executions and user activities. Integrate it with an AWS CloudTrail to enhance tracking of Redshift API calls.","Utilize the AWS Config for continuous monitoring of Amazon Redshift clusters' logging features to maintain compliance with regulations."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon Redshift</strong> keeps a record of all connections and user activities in your database, which is commonly known as database auditing. These logs are essential for security and troubleshooting purposes. They are stored in Amazon S3 buckets, which provide easy access and data security features for those who monitor database activities.</p>\n<p>Amazon Redshift creates three types of log files containing different information:</p>\n<p><strong> Connection log</strong> - records all authentication attempts, connections, and disconnections.</p>\n<p><strong>User log</strong> - keeps track of changes to database user definitions.</p>\n<p><strong>User activity log</strong> - records each query before it is run on the database.</p>\n<p>The user activity log is handy for troubleshooting since it provides information about the types of queries the users and the system perform in the database.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/de3092182a5c40e6395cf413e500b788d877a3f2080912fddade5d8630c86c2d.png\"></p>\n<p>While the connection and user log contain the same information as the system tables, the log files provide an easier way to retrieve and review the information. You need database permissions to query the system tables, while the log files rely on Amazon S3 permissions. Viewing the information in log files instead of querying system tables also helps reduce the impact of interacting with the database.</p>\n<p>AWS CloudTrail and Amazon Redshift integration offers detailed records of Redshift API calls, including API caller identity, time, source IP address, request parameters, and response elements. This integration provides a comprehensive audit trail that includes the actions taken within the Redshift cluster and API interactions that affect the cluster's configuration or data query. The built-in audit logging and CloudTrail integration create a robust monitoring solution that secures and audits a Redshift environment, ensuring compliance obligations are met, and data is safeguarded. This dual approach simplifies audit log management and enhances the visibility of operations within and around the Redshift clusters, making it easier to take a proactive stance on security and compliance.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Enable Amazon Redshift's built-in audit logging feature to capture detailed database transactions, including SQL query executions and user activities. Integrate it with an AWS CloudTrail to enhance tracking of Redshift API calls.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Parse Amazon CloudWatch Logs from the Redshift cluster using an AWS Lambda function. Store logs in Amazon OpenSearch Service for detailed analysis</strong></span> is incorrect because using AWS Lambda and integrating it with Amazon OpenSearch Service for parsing and storing logs can be complex and require custom development and maintenance. Moreover, CloudWatch Log entries for Redshift do not entirely capture SQL query executions, user activities, and other things that are fully covered by the built-in Audit Logging feature.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Integrate Amazon CloudWatch Logs with Amazon Redshift to collect Redshift SQL query logs. Utilize Amazon CloudWatch alarms and Amazon SNS to alert on any anomalies</strong></span> is incorrect. This approach mainly focuses on collecting SQL query logs and creating alerts for anomalies, which covers only a part of the monitoring requirements. However, it doesn't fulfill the need to document all user activities and changes made to databases and their objects in a way that is easy to audit for compliance purposes. Using the built-in Audit Logging feature is still the best option than using a combination of CloudWatch Logs and Alarms.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Utilize the AWS Config for continuous monitoring of Amazon Redshift clusters' logging features to maintain compliance with regulations</strong></span> is incorrect. AWS Config is a service for assessing and monitoring AWS resource configurations over time, but it doesn't capture detailed transactional audit logs in Redshift. It may not be suitable for the company's need to analyze production efficiency and inventory levels.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/logging-with-cloudtrail.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/logging-with-cloudtrail.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out our Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":22,"sourceNumber":32,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company uses an Amazon Kinesis data stream to manage its real-time data processing workloads. Recently, their consumer application on an EC2 instance started encountering <code>ProvisionedThroughputExceededException</code> errors.</p>\n<p>The company must resolve this issue to ensure that the read data calls to the stream consistently succeed and that their real-time data processing is not disrupted.</p>\n<p>Which actions could be taken to resolve the error? (Select TWO.)</p>","answers":["Increase the shard count of the Kinesis data stream.","Implement exponential backoff and retry logic in the application.","Activate the enhanced monitoring feature for the stream.","Enable the enhanced fan-out functionality on the stream.","Use a larger EC2 instance type for the Kinesis Data Streams application."],"correct":[0,1],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon Kinesis Data Streams</strong> is a real-time data streaming service offered by Amazon Web Services (AWS). It enables the collection, processing, and analysis of large streams of data records in real-time. A Kinesis data stream is a collection of data records that are continually and sequentially added to over time, much like a stream. Each data record in the stream is composed of a sequence number, a partition key, and a data blob, which is an immutable sequence of bytes. It is capable of handling real-time data from hundreds of thousands of sources at a massive scale. It’s commonly used for scenarios such as collecting and processing log and event data, real-time analytics, and real-time metrics and reporting.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/c59e1d966f1ed91fe21ba9dcf469b48032c30e88b1c7421ac56a648947158db5.png\" alt=\"\">Amazon Kinesis Data Streams uses shards, which are essentially independent streams of data within a Kinesis data stream. Each shard has a fixed rate of data it can handle — 1 MB/sec data input and 2 MB/sec data output. If the data rate exceeds the capacity of the current shards, you will receive a&nbsp;<code>ProvisionedThroughputExceededException</code>&nbsp;error. Therefore, increasing the number of shards in your data stream can increase the throughput of your data stream and resolve this error. This is a process known as resharding, which involves splitting or merging shards to adjust the total capacity of the stream.</p>\n<p>When a&nbsp;<code>ProvisionedThroughputExceededException</code>&nbsp;error occurs, it means that the application is reading data too quickly from the stream. Implementing a backoff strategy, where the application waits for a short period before trying the request again, can help manage the rate of data retrieval and avoid exceeding the read-throughput limit. This is a common strategy for handling rate-limited APIs and services, and it’s particularly important when dealing with real-time data processing workloads where maintaining a steady flow of data is crucial.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span>-&nbsp;<strong>Increase the shard count of the Kinesis data stream.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>-&nbsp;Implement exponential backoff and retry logic in the application.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Activate the enhanced monitoring feature for the stream</span></strong>&nbsp;is incorrect because while enhanced monitoring provides additional metrics for the stream, it does not directly resolve the&nbsp;<code>ProvisionedThroughputExceededException</code>&nbsp;error. This error is related to the stream’s capacity, not its monitoring.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Enable the enhanced fan-out functionality on the stream</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect.&nbsp;While enhanced fan-out can improve the consumer-side experience by providing dedicated throughput per consumer, it does not directly address the overall capacity quotas of the data stream. It's more about optimizing the consumption pattern rather than increasing the stream's capacity to accept writes or aggregate reads without exceeding provisioned throughput.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Use a larger EC2 instance type for the Kinesis Data Streams application</span></strong>&nbsp;is incorrect because the size of the EC2 instance does not directly affect the capacity of the Kinesis data stream. The&nbsp;<code>ProvisionedThroughputExceededException</code>&nbsp;error is related to the stream’s capacity, not the size of the EC2 instance running the application.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/kinesis-using-sdk-java-resharding.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/kinesis-using-sdk-java-resharding.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/troubleshooting-consumers.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/troubleshooting-consumers.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Kinesis Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":23,"sourceNumber":33,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company has established a data pipeline to handle large volumes of data. The pipeline consists of an AWS Glue job that pulls data from a source S3 bucket, transforms it, and sends the result to a target S3 bucket.</p>\n<p>A data engineer must configure permissions for the Glue job to access these S3 buckets, which reside in the same AWS account.</p>\n<p>Which of the following options would meet the requirements with the LEAST amount of configuration effort?</p>","answers":["Create an IAM role with necessary S3 permissions and assign this role to the AWS Glue service.","Create an IAM user for AWS Glue and attach policies for S3 access.","Use Amazon S3 bucket policies to grant access to AWS Glue.","Use S3 Access Control Lists (ACLs) to manage permissions."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>AWS Glue is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy to prepare and load your data for analytics. To perform its operations, AWS Glue needs to interact with other AWS services on your behalf. This is achieved by using an IAM role that has the necessary permissions. When you create an IAM role for AWS Glue, you grant your IAM role permissions that AWS Glue can assume when calling other services on your behalf. This includes access to Amazon S3 for any sources, targets, scripts, and temporary directories that you use with AWS Glue.</p>\n<p>By creating a new IAM service role specifically for AWS Glue and attaching the necessary policies to this role, you can grant the necessary permissions to AWS Glue without affecting other services or users. This approach also aligns with the principle of least privilege, which is a best practice in IAM. This principle encourages you to grant only the permissions required to perform a task.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/863c12bae4209fa7f1592de2df2a1c0f26b29e533b57bee8c2037b5f7c3e23df.jpg\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>This approach allows you to separate the permissions for the source and destination S3 buckets by attaching different policies to the IAM role. For example, you could attach a policy that grants read access to the source bucket and a separate policy that grants write access to the destination bucket. This way, you can meet the requirement of separating the permissions for the source and destination S3 buckets.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Create an IAM role with necessary S3 permissions and assign this role to the AWS Glue service</strong><strong>.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create an IAM user for AWS Glue and attach policies for S3 access</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Glue jobs are designed to assume an IAM role. It's not possible to assign IAM user's credentials to AWS Glue.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use Amazon S3 bucket policies to grant access to AWS Glue</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect.&nbsp;While this is a viable approach, using S3 bucket policies to grant access to the AWS Glue service requires separate bucket policies to be configured for both the source and target S3 buckets. With an IAM role, you can centralize the permission management, simplifying the configuration process.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use S3 Access Control Lists (ACLs) to manage permissions</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because ACLs are less flexible and harder to manage than IAM roles. ACLs provide a broad level of access control, but they don’t provide the same level of granularity as IAM roles. This can make it difficult to adhere to the principle of least privilege, as you might end up granting more permissions than necessary. Additionally, managing ACLs can be complex and error-prone, especially if you have many S3 buckets or objects.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/create-an-iam-role.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/create-an-iam-role.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/set-up-iam.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/set-up-iam.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this&nbsp;AWS Identity and Access Management Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-identity-and-access-management-iam/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-identity-and-access-management-iam/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":24,"sourceNumber":34,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company is leveraging Amazon Redshift for its data warehousing needs. The data engineering team is tasked with running an exploratory data analysis (EDA) to prepare the data for a predictive model. The team needs to query relevant information from the Redshift cluster and export the results for further processing. The connection details for the Redshift cluster must be securely stored and retrieved.</p>\n<p>Which of the following approaches would meet the requirements? (SelectTWO.)</p>","answers":["Leverage Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler to query the necessary information from the Redshift cluster. After the exploratory data analysis (EDA) is complete, export the results to Amazon S3 for further processing.","<span>Configure AWS Secrets Manager to store the connection details of the Redshift cluster. Use the <code>AWS SDK for pandas</code> to retrieve the secret values and connect to the Redshift cluster.</span>","Use AWS Lambda to run the EDA queries on the Redshift cluster. Store the results in DynamoDB for further processing.","Configure AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store to store the connection details of the Redshift cluster. Use Amazon Redshift Data API to connect to the Redshift cluster.","Launch an Amazon EMR cluster and run an Apache Spark job to query relevant information from the Redshift cluster. Export the results to Amazon S3 for further processing."],"correct":[0,1],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler</strong> reduces the time it takes to aggregate and prepare data for machine learning (ML) from weeks to minutes. With SageMaker Data Wrangler, you can simplify the process of data preparation and feature engineering, and complete each step of the data preparation workflow, including data selection, cleansing, exploration, and visualization from a single visual interface. After the exploratory data analysis (EDA) is complete, exporting the results to Amazon S3 for further processing aligns with the requirements of securely storing and retrieving data for predictive modeling. Amazon S3 has a simple web services interface that you can use to store and retrieve any amount of data at any time from anywhere on the web.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/24f0181ef7a422564f0b52039c384533fc25682e257ffa7637b4dcb3d21991a1.jpg\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>AWS Secrets Manager protects access to your applications, services, and IT resources without the upfront investment and ongoing maintenance costs of operating your own infrastructure. This service enables you to easily rotate, manage, and retrieve database credentials, API keys, and other secrets throughout their lifecycle.</p>\n<p><strong>AWS SDK</strong>&nbsp;<strong>for</strong>&nbsp;<strong>pandas</strong>&nbsp;(previously known as <strong>awswrangler</strong>) is an open-source Python project that bridges the pandas library with AWS data and analytics services, effectively expanding pandas' capabilities into the AWS cloud ecosystem. This tool makes it straightforward to work with various AWS services (e.g, Athena, Glue, Redshift, Secrets Manager, S3) directly from pandas DataFrames. This includes support for a range of file formats such as Parquet, CSV, JSON, and EXCEL.&nbsp;By leveraging the AWS SDK for pandas, developers can write more efficient and streamlined code for a wide range of tasks, such as exploratory data analysis (EDA), data transformation, and loading (ETL) processes.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span><strong>-&nbsp;Leverage Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler to query the necessary information from the Redshift cluster. After the exploratory data analysis (EDA) is complete, export the results to Amazon S3 for further processing.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>-&nbsp;Configure AWS Secrets Manager to store the connection details of the Redshift cluster. Use the <code>AWS SDK for pandas</code> to retrieve the secret values and connect to the Redshift cluster.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use AWS Lambda to run the EDA queries on the Redshift cluster. Store the results in DynamoDB for further processing</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because DynamoDB is a NoSQL database service, which is not suitable for storing EDA results that are typically structured and relational in nature.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store to store the connection details of the Redshift cluster. Use Amazon Redshift Data API to connect to the Redshift cluster</strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect. While you can use the AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store to store sensitive data like database credentials, it does not have a native integration with Redshift Data API, unlike Secrets Manager. In AWS, Secrets Manager is more preferable for storing database credentials due to its automated secrets rotation and tight integration with AWS database services.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Launch an Amazon EMR cluster and run an Apache Spark job to query relevant information from the Redshift cluster. Export the results to Amazon S3 for further processing&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect. While EMR and Spark are powerful tools for big data processing, they are not designed to interact directly with Amazon Redshift for the purpose of running EDA.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/data-wrangler.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/data-wrangler.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/import-data-from-cross-account-amazon-redshift-in-amazon-sagemaker-data-wrangler-for-exploratory-data-analysis-and-data-preparation/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/import-data-from-cross-account-amazon-redshift-in-amazon-sagemaker-data-wrangler-for-exploratory-data-analysis-and-data-preparation/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/using-the-amazon-redshift-data-api-to-interact-with-amazon-redshift-clusters/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/using-the-amazon-redshift-data-api-to-interact-with-amazon-redshift-clusters/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out these Amazon SageMaker and AWS Secrets Manager Cheat Sheets:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-secrets-manager/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-secrets-manager/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":25,"sourceNumber":37,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A software company is rehosting a high-volume data-processing application running on-premises to AWS Lambda. Currently, the application has access to 200 GB of data stored on a local disk for its operations. The company prefers to migrate the application with minimal changes to the code.</p>\n<p>Which approach would best meet these requirements?</p>","answers":["<span>Temporarily store the reference dataset in the Lambda function's <code>/tmp</code> directory.</span>","Transfer the reference dataset to an Amazon S3 bucket and set up a Lambda function for data interaction using Amazon S3 API.","Launch a new file system in the Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) console and upload the reference data. Create an access point and configure the Lambda function for data access.","Implement a cluster and domain in Amazon OpenSearch Service and integrate data using Logstash. Modify the Lambda function to communicate with this environment for data access and manipulation."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS)</strong> is an easy-to-use and scalable file storage solution for AWS Cloud services and on-premises resources. It can scale up to petabytes of data without affecting your applications and automatically adjusts its size as files are added or removed. This makes it a highly reliable and available storage option for a wide range of workloads and applications, including AWS Lambda functions.</p>\n<p>An<strong> Amazon EFS access point</strong> is a way to manage application access to shared datasets within an EFS file system. With access points, you can configure a user, group, and root directory for all file system requests made through that access point. This ensures that access control is enforced at the file system level.</p>\n<p><strong>AWS Lambda</strong> enables serverless execution of code, scaling automatically to meet demand from a few requests to thousands per second.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/2b14cec07f3a534b9e9e33587f128225f3a16c79e70fb5d6f129df55d4125387.jpg\"></p>\n<p>Lambda functions can seamlessly mount an EFS file system to the function's file system namespace using an access point. This setup allows Lambda functions to read and write files just as if they were on a local file system but with EFS's scalability, flexibility, and performance. This method requires minimal changes to existing code that interacts with mounted volumes, making it the most suitable option for migrating data-intensive workloads with minimal code refactoring.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<strong> <span>Launch a new file system in the Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) console and upload the reference data. Create an access point and configure the Lambda function for data access.</span></strong></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Temporarily store the reference dataset in the Lambda function's <code>/tmp</code> directory</strong></span> is incorrect. The <code>/tmp</code> directory in AWS Lambda provides a temporary storage space for the function's execution environment. Data in this directory exists only for the duration of the function's runtime and is then lost. Additionally, with its limited size (512 MB to 10 GB), it's unsuitable for workloads that require access to 200 GB of data.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Transfer the reference dataset to an Amazon S3 bucket and set up a Lambda function for data interaction using Amazon S3 API</strong></span> is incorrect. While a Lambda function can read and write data to and from an S3 bucket, implementing this solution would require significant modifications to the existing code that accesses data through a mounted volume. Specifically, you would need to modify the code to use the S3 data API with the AWS SDK, which would not meet the requirement to minimize the number of code modifications.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Implement a cluster and domain in Amazon OpenSearch Service and integrate data using Logstash. Modify the Lambda function to communicate with this environment for data access and manipulation</strong></span> is incorrect. Amazon OpenSearch Service (successor to Amazon Elasticsearch Service) offers powerful search capabilities over large volumes of data. While it's an excellent service for search and analytics, using it for data-intensive workloads that originally rely on mounted data volumes would necessitate substantial changes to the application logic, particularly for workloads not primarily focused on search or analytics.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-filesystem.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-filesystem.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/efs/latest/ug/efs-access-points.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/efs/latest/ug/efs-access-points.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out these Amazon EFS and AWS Lambda Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-efs/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-efs/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":26,"sourceNumber":38,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineer is tasked with regularly updating their Amazon Redshift data warehouse. Every month, the data engineer receives a large batch of new data. This data often contains updates to existing records, and the goal is to ensure that the data in Redshift is always up-to-date while minimizing the impact on query performance.</p>\n<p>Which strategy should be chosen to ensure efficient data ingestion?</p>","answers":["<span>Load the updated data into a temporary table using the <code>INSERT</code> command. Run an AWS Glue job that applies the Drop Duplicates transformation to remove any duplicate records in the temporary table.</span>","<span>Use the <code>INSERT</code> command to load the updated data into a temporary table. Then, run the <code>UPSERT</code> command to insert new records and update existing ones in the main table.</span>","<span>Create a temporary table for the updated data. Use the <code>MERGE</code> operation on the temporary table and the main data warehouse. When no match is detected, use the <code>INSERT</code> operation to insert values from the temporary table into the main data warehouse. When a match is detected, use the <code>UPDATE</code> operation to update the main data warehouse with values from the temporary table.</span>","<span>Load the updated data into a temporary table using the <code>INSERT</code> command. Then, use the <code>DELETE</code> command to remove any records in the main table that also exist in the temporary table. After that, use the <code>INSERT</code> command to insert new records from the temporary table into the main table.</span>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Amazon Redshift is a cloud-based data warehouse that you can use to perform data analysis. One of the features of Amazon Redshift is the support for the&nbsp;<code>MERGE</code>&nbsp;operation, which uses a temporary staging table. This operation is particularly useful when you have a large batch of updated data that often contains updates to existing records, and you want to ensure that the data in Redshift is always up-to-date while minimizing the impact on query performance.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/1a40ec95d994eff98c367e27960b40ba475811b06bda457f6cfb73ecfc604f64.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>The&nbsp;<code>MERGE</code>&nbsp;operation works by comparing the records in two tables based on a specified match condition. When the&nbsp;<code>MERGE</code>&nbsp;operation detects a match between the records in the two tables, it performs an&nbsp;<code>UPDATE</code>&nbsp;operation. This means that the existing records in the main table are updated with the values from the matching records in the temporary table.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, when the&nbsp;<code>MERGE</code>&nbsp;operation does not detect a match, it performs an&nbsp;<code>INSERT</code>&nbsp;operation. This means that the new records from the temporary table are inserted into the main table.</p>\n<p>This strategy of using the&nbsp;<code>MERGE</code>&nbsp;operation ensures efficient data ingestion because it minimizes the number of separate operations that need to be performed. Instead of having to perform&nbsp;<code>INSERT</code>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<code>UPDATE</code>&nbsp;operations separately, you can perform them both at once using the&nbsp;<code>MERGE</code>&nbsp;operation. This also minimizes the impact on query performance, as the database doesn’t have to process separate&nbsp;<code>INSERT</code>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<code>UPDATE</code>&nbsp;commands.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Create a temporary table for the updated data. Use the <code>MERGE</code> operation on the temporary table and the main data warehouse. When no match is detected, use the <code>INSERT</code> operation to insert values from the temporary table into the main data warehouse. When a match is detected, use the <code>UPDATE</code> operation to update the main data warehouse with values from the temporary table</strong></span>&nbsp;is correct because&nbsp;it involves creating a temporary table and using the&nbsp;<code>MERGE</code>&nbsp;operation. When no match is detected, the&nbsp;<code>INSERT</code>&nbsp;operation is used to add new records from the temporary table to the main data warehouse. When a match is detected, the&nbsp;<code>UPDATE</code>&nbsp;operation is used to update the existing records in the main data warehouse with the new data. This ensures that the data in Redshift is always up-to-date while minimizing the impact on query performance.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Load the updated data into a temporary table using the&nbsp;<code>INSERT</code>&nbsp;command. Run an AWS Glue job that applies the Drop Duplicates transformation to remove any duplicate records in the temporary table&nbsp;</strong></span>is&nbsp;incorrect.&nbsp;While loading the data into a temporary table and using an AWS Glue job to remove duplicates might ensure that the data in the temporary table is unique, this strategy does not account for handling updates to existing records in the main table. Additional steps would be required to compare and merge this data back into the main table.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use the&nbsp;<code>INSERT</code>&nbsp;command to load the updated data into a temporary table. Then, run the&nbsp;<code>UPSERT</code>&nbsp;command to insert new records and update existing ones in the main table</strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect because&nbsp;Amazon Redshift does not natively support the&nbsp;<code>UPSERT</code>&nbsp;command. However, you could achieve similar functionality using a combination of Amazon services and big data frameworks. For instance, Amazon EMR, a managed service that runs big data frameworks including Hudi, supports record-level operations, including&nbsp;<code>INSERT</code>,&nbsp;<code>UPDATE</code>,&nbsp;<code>UPSERT</code>, and&nbsp;<code>DELETE</code>.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Load the updated data into a temporary table using the <code>INSERT</code> command. Then, use the <code>DELETE</code> command to remove any records in the main table that also exist in the temporary table. After that, use the <code>INSERT</code> command to insert new records from the temporary table into the main table</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect.&nbsp;This approach involves deleting existing records that match those in the new batch before inserting the new data. This strategy can be very disruptive and might lead to data unavailability or integrity issues if not managed carefully.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/maximize-data-ingestion-and-reporting-performance-on-amazon-redshift/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/maximize-data-ingestion-and-reporting-performance-on-amazon-redshift/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_MERGE.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_MERGE.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":27,"sourceNumber":39,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A Philippines-based company ships orders globally. These orders are placed in an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) queue. The company has an AWS Step Functions workflow that exclusively processes international orders. The workflow currently uses a <code>Choice</code> state to route international orders, but it still starts for every message, incurring unnecessary cost for domestic ('PH') orders. This workflow should only be executed when an order's data does not contain the 'PH' country code.</p>\n<p>Which of the following options would satisfy the requirements in the LEAST amount of development effort?</p>","answers":["<span>Create an Amazon EventBridge rule with an event pattern that matches the <code>SendMessage</code> SQS actions. Select the Step Functions workflow as the target. Implement a filtering logic in the Step functions workflow.</span>","Filter orders based on the SQS queue length, considering longer queues to have more relevant orders.","Create an AWS Lambda function with an event source mapping for SQS. Configure an event pattern that filters non-PH orders. Write the function’s code to invoke the Step Functions workflow.","Create a pipe in Amazon EventBridge Pipes with the SQS queue as the source. Configure an event pattern that filters non-PH orders. Select the Step Functions workflow as the target."],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Amazon EventBridge Pipes connects sources to targets. Pipes are intended for point-to-point integrations between supported sources and targets, with support for advanced transformations and enrichment. It reduces the need for specialized knowledge and integration code when developing event-driven architectures, fostering consistency across your company’s applications. To set up a pipe, you choose the source, add optional filtering, define optional enrichment, and choose the target for the event data.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/650af49b6e2b401d8e32338bd4e536ddd54c1fa60c2a9ec6fb0d362239739ffb.jpg\" alt=\"Event filtering pattern matching SQS events to a Step Function target\"></p>\n<p>In this scenario, the goal is to only run the Step Functions workflow for orders not marked with the 'PH' country code, avoiding unnecessary executions. By using Amazon EventBridge Pipes with an \"anything-but\" event filter, we can directly target only those orders. This setup filters out orders before they trigger the workflow, ensuring that only international orders proceed for processing.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<span><strong> Create a pipe in Amazon EventBridge Pipes with the SQS queue as the source. Configure an event pattern that filters non-PH orders. Select the Step Functions workflow as the target.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create an Amazon EventBridge rule with an event pattern that matches the <code>SendMessage</code> SQS actions. Select the Step Functions workflow as the target. Implement a filtering logic in the Step functions workflow</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. With this approach, the workflow would be triggered for each order, regardless of the country code, leading to unnecessary executions of the workflow. Moreover, relying on the <code>SendMessage</code> action in Amazon EventBridge for processing orders may not be simply reliable due to potential delays or the asynchronous nature of the message delivery system in Amazon SQS.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Filter orders based on the SQS queue length, considering longer queues to have more relevant orders</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because&nbsp;the length of the SQS queue (the number of messages in the queue) is just not a reliable indicator of an order's relevance to the workflow. Also, this approach will not filter orders containing non-PH country codes.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create an AWS Lambda function with an event source mapping for SQS. Configure an event pattern that filters non-PH orders. Write the function’s code to invoke the Step Functions workflow.</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. While feasible, this solution demands extra development effort because it involves using a Lambda function as an intermediary to invoke the Step Functions. This is in contrast to using EventBridge Pipes, which provides a more straightforward approach.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/welcome.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/welcome.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-pipes.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-pipes.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon EventBridge Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-eventbridge/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-eventbridge/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":28,"sourceNumber":40,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p><span>A data engineer uses an on-premises application to process messages from an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) queue. The application has retry logic implemented to address message processing failures.</span></p>\n<p><span>However, due to a network outage, many messages failed to process and ended up in the dead-letter queues (DLQ). The engineer wants to mitigate the impact of similar network disruptions in the future.</span></p>\n<p><span>Which solution should the engineer implement to meet the requirement?</span></p>","answers":["<span>Increase the <code>delaySeconds</code> setting for the SQS queue.</span>","<span>Decrease the <code>visibilityTimeout</code> setting for the SQS queue.</span>","<span>Increase the <code>maxReceiveCount</code> setting for the SQS queue.</span>","<span>Adjust the <code>receiveMessageWaitTimeSeconds</code> setting for the SQS queue.</span>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon SQS</strong> is a fully managed message queuing service that provides a virtual queue acting as a buffer between the components of an application. It enables you to decouple and scale microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications.</p>\n<p><strong><code>DelaySeconds</code></strong> setting refers to the delay period for a delay queue. This sets how long any messages sent to the queue remain invisible to consumers after being added to the queue. The default is 0 seconds, and the maximum is 15 minutes.</p>\n<p><code><strong>VisibilityTimeout</strong></code> is the period of time during which Amazon SQS prevents other consuming components from receiving and processing a message after it has been received from a queue (but not yet deleted). This helps prevent a message from being received and processed more than once.</p>\n<p><code><strong>MaxReceiveCount</strong></code> refers to the number of times a message can be received from the queue before being deleted. When the limit is reached, the message will be sent to the dead letter queue. The default Maximum received is 10.</p>\n<p><code><strong>ReceiveMessageWaitTimeSeconds</strong></code> is an optional parameter that sets the duration (in seconds) for waiting for a message to arrive in the queue.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/7192849758d8a034e8fd7ad67d196fa7296d66c604dfbee3a7e03bef518746de.png\" alt=\"Dead-letter queue\"></p>\n<p>To reduce the number of premature messages sent to the DLQ, the engineer should increase the <code>MaxReceiveCount</code> setting for the SQS queue (In the AWS Management Console, it is the same with the option labeled \"Maximum receives\"). By increasing the <code>MaxReceiveCount</code>, it allows the on-premises application to retry processing messages more times before they are sent to the DLQ. This gives the application more opportunities to successfully process messages even during brief network issues.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <strong><span>Increase the <code>MaxReceiveCount</code> setting for the SQS queue.</span></strong></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Increase the <code>DelaySeconds</code> setting for the SQS queue</strong></span> is incorrect because delaying message delivery won't effectively handle transient failures. It merely postpones the delivery of messages without providing additional attempts for message processing.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Decrease the <code>VisibilityTimeout</code> setting for the SQS queue</strong></span> is incorrect. Lowering this timeout can typically lead to messages becoming available to other consumers too quickly before the initial processing attempt can be retried, exacerbating the problem.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Adjust the <code>ReceiveMessageWaitTimeSeconds</code> setting for the SQS queue</strong></span> is incorrect. While long polling can primarily reduce the number of empty responses from SQS by waiting for messages to arrive, it does not influence the retry behavior or the movement of messages to the DLQ.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-dead-letter-queues.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-dead-letter-queues.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/working-with-messages.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/working-with-messages.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon SQS Cheat Sheet: </strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sqs/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sqs/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":29,"sourceNumber":41,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company is developing a data processing pipeline for their e-commerce platform. The pipeline requires the execution of Python code every Monday at 9:00 AM UTC to analyze customer behavior data stored in S3 buckets that serve as a data lake. This data includes information such as page views, product clicks, and purchase history. The solution should prioritize operational efficiency.</p>\n<p>Which of the following options would be most suitable for this scenario? (Select TWO.)</p>","answers":["Use AWS Lambda to execute the Python code.","Use Amazon EventBridge to execute the Python code every Monday at 9:00 AM UTC.","Use AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK) to define cloud infrastructure in code and provision it through AWS CloudFormation.","Use AWS Cloud9 to write, run, and debug the Python code.","Use AWS CloudShell to execute the Python code."],"correct":[0,1],"explanation":"<p>AWS Lambda is a serverless computing service that lets you run your code without provisioning or managing servers. In the given scenario, you can upload your Python code to AWS Lambda, which takes care of all the capacity, scaling, patching, and administration of the infrastructure to run your code. This allows you to focus on analyzing customer behavior data rather than managing infrastructure, which aligns with the requirement to prioritize operational efficiency.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/da0fad61a33a04df8d1673d7c89ef751d002dfa304ed67c1955a590c0354d995.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Amazon EventBridge is a serverless event bus service that makes it easy to connect applications together using data from your own applications, Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) applications, and AWS services. You can set up a rule in EventBridge to trigger your AWS Lambda function every Monday at 9:00 AM UTC. This way, your Python code gets executed automatically at the specified time each week.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><strong><span>- Use AWS Lambda to execute the Python code.</span></strong></p>\n<p><strong><span>- Use Amazon EventBridge to execute the Python code every Monday at 9:00 AM UTC.</span></strong></p>\n<p>The option that says:<span><strong>&nbsp;Use AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK) to define cloud infrastructure in code and provision it through AWS CloudFormation</strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect. While CDK can be used to create the infrastructure needed for the pipeline (including Lambda functions and EventBridge rules), it doesn't directly execute Python code as part of a data processing pipeline. It's more about setting up the infrastructure.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use AWS Cloud9 to write, run, and debug the Python code</strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect&nbsp;because AWS Cloud9 is just a cloud-based integrated development environment (IDE) that lets you write, run, and debug your code with just a browser. It can't be used to execute Python code on schedule as part of a data processing pipeline.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use AWS CloudShell to execute the Python code</strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect&nbsp;because AWS CloudShell is simply a browser-based shell&nbsp;intended for interactive use and does not support scheduling or automation.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/\">https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-what-is.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-what-is.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Lambda Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":30,"sourceNumber":42,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company that provides a popular music streaming service is experiencing slow query performance when its data team tries to analyze user listening habits. The data team needs to perform complex queries on large tables containing billions of rows of data, which include user demographics, song metadata, and user activity logs.</p>\n<p>The current infrastructure is struggling to handle the load, causing delays in generating insights. The data team routinely executes a <code>VACUUM FULL</code> operation to maintain table health, but this has not resolved the performance bottlenecks for these repeatedly executed complex queries.</p>\n<p>Which of the following would meet the requirements?</p>","answers":["Use the built-in Aggregate View.","Set up a Custom scalar user-defined function (UDF) using either a SQL SELECT clause or a Python program.","Integrate Amazon ElastiCache to cache query results.","Implement a Materialized View in Amazon Redshift."],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p><strong>Materialized views</strong> in Amazon Redshift provide a way to handle complex queries on large datasets. A materialized view contains a precomputed result set, based on an SQL query over one or more base tables. This means that the results of the query are calculated and stored when the view is created, rather than being calculated at query time. This can significantly speed up query performance on large datasets.</p>\n<p>Materialized views are especially useful for speeding up queries that are predictable and repeated. Instead of performing resource-intensive queries against large tables (such as aggregates or multiple joins), applications can query a materialized view and retrieve a precomputed result set. For example, consider the scenario where a set of queries is used to populate dashboards, such as Amazon QuickSight. This use case is ideal for a materialized view because the queries are predictable and repeated over and over again.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/ad9c1ac30239981d1b59c72384abcfa18aa36ead6ba1e429744c6b3583fbd24d.png\" alt=\"Materialized view aggregating total sales by event from source tables\"></p>\n<p>In addition, Amazon Redshift can automatically rewrite these queries to use materialized views, even when the query doesn’t explicitly reference a materialized view. This improves query performance because many computation steps can be skipped and the precomputed results returned directly. Unlike a simple cache, many materialized views can be incrementally refreshed when DML changes are applied on the underlying (base) tables and can be used by other similar queries, not just the query used to create the materialized view.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<strong>&nbsp;</strong><span><strong><span>Implement a Materialized View in Amazon Redshift.</span></strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:<strong><span> Use the built-in Aggregate View</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect. Aggregate view is simply a feature in Amazon DynamoDB, not Amazon Redshift. They represent all the tables in a data model, including table column names, sample data, and all global secondary indexes associated with the table. This does not inherently improve the performance of complex queries on large datasets.</p>\n<p>The option that says:<strong><span> Set up a Custom scalar user-defined function (UDF) using either a SQL SELECT clause or a Python program</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect. While user-defined functions in Amazon Redshift can be used to encapsulate complex operations, they do not inherently improve the performance of querying large datasets. A UDF executes on a single row and then returns a single value. It doesn’t benefit from parallel execution and can’t return a table or a result set.</p>\n<p>The option that says:<strong><span> Integrate Amazon ElastiCache to cache query results</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect. Amazon ElastiCache is an in-memory key-value store that sits between your application and the data store (database) that it accesses. It can be used for caching to improve application and database speed. However, it is not designed to handle complex queries on large datasets like the ones mentioned in the scenario. It is more suited only for scenarios where the same data is being read repeatedly.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/optimize-your-amazon-redshift-query-performance-with-automated-materialized-views/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/optimize-your-amazon-redshift-query-performance-with-automated-materialized-views/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":31,"sourceNumber":43,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A retail chain compiles daily transaction records from multiple store locations in a Comma-Separated Values (CSV) format and uploads them to Amazon S3. A data engineer uses Amazon Athena to generate an analysis of these records.</p>\n<p>Only a subset of columns is typically queried, yet Athena scans the entire table each time. The data engineer must create a solution to reduce the volume of data involved in a query.</p>\n<p>Which approach would meet the requirements?</p>","answers":["Use AWS Glue Schema Registry to strip any unused columns.","<span>Organize the S3 files into a <code>/year/month/day</code> structure. Then, create a partitioning schema via the <code>PARTITIONED BY</code> clause in the <code>CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE</code> command.</span>","Divide the dataset into multiple compressed Gzip files, each approximately 1 GB in size, before storage.","Execute the query in an Athena workgroup and apply a specified per-query control limit."],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon Athena</strong> is a user-friendly service that uses standard SQL queries to analyze data stored in Amazon S3. With Athena, you can analyze data in S3 without the need to load it into a separate database.</p>\n<p>Partitioning data in Amazon S3 and utilizing Amazon Athena for querying can significantly reduce the amount of data scanned, lowering costs. Partitioning enables Athena to scan only the relevant partitions of data for a query rather than the entire dataset. This method is highly recommended for large datasets stored in S3, where queries often target a subset of the data based on specific columns like date, region, or department.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/740b2cadf01db881a98b3e591ad21d58f371827eb13c971003de8eb42bb40428.jpg\"></p>\n<p>By organizing the CSV files in an S3 bucket with a <code>/year/month/day</code> structure, you can create partitions based on these fields. When creating an external table in Athena, The <code>CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE</code> statement defines the table structure, columns, and data types. The <code>EXTERNAL</code> keyword specifies that the table is external to Athena and points to the data location in S3.</p>\n<p>The <code>PARTITIONED BY</code> clause can be used to define a partitioning schema. This divides the table horizontally based on partition columns like date, product, etc.</p>\n<p>When queries use partition columns in the filter criteria, Athena can prune partitions to skip irrelevant data locations and reduce the amount of data scanned.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<strong> <span>Organize the S3 files into a <code>/year/month/day</code> structure. Then, create a partitioning schema via the <code>PARTITIONED BY</code> clause in the <code>CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE</code> command.</span></strong></p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Use AWS Glue Schema Registry to strip any unused columns</span></strong> is incorrect. AWS Glue Schema Registry is primarily used for managing and controlling data schemas. It does not have the capability to remove or strip columns from the actual data stored in Amazon S3 or other data sources.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Execute the query in an Athena workgroup and apply a specified per-query control limit</span></strong> is incorrect. Per-query control limits just set a boundary on the total amount of data that can be scanned. It won't reduce the amount of data processed by Athena. If the limit is set too low, it may result in incomplete or incorrect query results, as the query may be terminated prematurely before scanning the necessary data.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Divide the dataset into multiple compressed Gzip files, each approximately 1 GB in size, before storage</span></strong> is incorrect. While compression can reduce storage costs, Athena still needs to scan the entire compressed dataset.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/ctas-partitioning-and-bucketing.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/ctas-partitioning-and-bucketing.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/create-table.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/create-table.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":32,"sourceNumber":44,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineer needs to perform data analytics on datasets that originate from various sources. The engineer wants to use Apache Spark to process these datasets and interactively visualize the results.</p>\n<p>Which solution will meet this requirement with LEAST operational overhead?</p>","answers":["Use Amazon EMR Studio notebooks","Use Athena query editor","Use AWS Glue Studio","Use Athena Notebooks"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon Athena Notebooks</strong> is an interactive coding environment where you can write and execute Apache Spark code using Python. Athena notebooks allow chaining together SQL queries, calculations, and visualizations.</p>\n<p><strong>Apache Spark</strong> is a distributed processing framework used for big data analytics. It utilizes in-memory computing techniques for fast performance on large datasets.</p>\n<p>Using <strong>Apache Spark in Amazon Athena Notebooks</strong> allows you to run Spark interactively on data from various sources without having to provision or manage infrastructure. You can write Python code in Athena notebooks to query data, perform Spark transformations and actions, and visualize results. Athena handles the automatic scaling of resources for your Spark jobs based on requirements.</p>\n<p>Data can be queried directly from data lakes in Amazon S3, databases, etc., using Athena's metadata cataloging.</p>\n<p>Notebooks provide a Jupyter-like interface to develop and run Spark code and applications.</p>\n<p>Resources are serverless - you only pay for the actual notebook/query executions without any upfront costs.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/5d447c2bf762c64585ee29ac7891241cd741ca6a942147653a57cc5dfa6c8d19.png\"></p>\n<p>Based on the provided requirements, the most cost-effective solution for the data engineer to implement would be Amazon Athena Notebooks. Amazon Athena is a serverless solution, eliminating the need for infrastructure provisioning and management. It enables querying data directly from various sources, eliminating the need for data migration and satisfying the requirement to analyze data from different sources. Additionally, Athena's integration with Apache Spark allows for interactive data analytics and visualization through Python notebooks, chaining together SQL queries, calculations, and visualizations for exploration purposes. Athena's automatic scaling of compute resources ensures faster notebook execution without the hassle of resource sizing. Most importantly, with Athena, the data engineer only pays for the actual queries and notebooks run, with no upfront costs or minimum commitments, making it highly cost-effective for the required interactive analysis.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<strong> <span>Use Athena Notebooks.</span></strong></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong> Use Amazon EMR Studio notebooks&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect because, although EMR notebooks support Apache Spark and interactive data analysis, they require provisioning and managing an EMR cluster, which can be more costly than a serverless solution like Athena.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use Athena query editor</strong></span> is incorrect because the Athena query editor is designed for running SQL queries and does not directly support interactive data visualization or analytics using Apache Spark.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use AWS Glue Studio</strong></span> is incorrect because AWS Glue Studio is primarily used for creating, running, and monitoring ETL jobs, not for interactive data analysis and visualization with Apache Spark.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/spark/\">https://aws.amazon.com/athena/spark/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":33,"sourceNumber":45,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company is moving its AWS Glue DataBrew project to a new AWS account managed by a new data engineering team. This requires stopping DataBrew activities in the existing account and removing certain configurations that enabled Glue DataBrew to connect to the company’s Amazon RDS databases, EC2 instances, and other VPC resources.</p>\n<p>The new data engineering team is responsible for cutting off the previous DataBrew connection. Moreover, the team must thoroughly understand the appropriate procedures to ensure a seamless and secure transition.</p>\n<p>Which of the following should the data engineering team implement to satisfy the given requirement?</p>","answers":["Detach the security group from all associated VPC resources and delete any security group rules within the VPC that reference the security group.","Reconfigure the VPC to utilize a new security group for DataBrew connectivity before deleting the old one.","Disable deletion protection on the security group in AWS Security Hub to allow its removal.","Delete the Elastic Network Interface (ENI) associated with AWS Glue DataBrew before removing the security group."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><strong>Security groups&nbsp;</strong>are crucial to Amazon Web Services (AWS) network security, especially for resources deployed in a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC). It acts as a virtual firewall for instances, allowing control of inbound and outbound traffic at the instance level. Unlike network access control lists (ACLs), which operate at the subnet level, security groups are directly associated with individual resources such as Amazon EC2 instances and AWS Glue DataBrew services that work within a VPC.</p>\n<p>A security group's rules are responsible for the traffic allowed to and from resources associated with it. When a new instance or service is launched within a VPC, it can be related to one or more security groups, which then govern traffic flow to and from the resource. Managing these security groups effectively is crucial for maintaining the security and integrity of the resources within a VPC.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/404cd30bfe98dec4655f583d729e67bf70c50665fff13980f6975d7237dd5135.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>For the scenario described, the task is removing a security group associated explicitly with AWS Glue DataBrew operations as part of a migration to a new AWS account. This process involves a few critical steps to ensure the removal does not disrupt existing operations or compromise security. First, detaching the security group from any resources within the VPC is necessary. This step ensures that no active resources are left unprotected or inadvertently exposed due to the security group's removal. Following this, it's also essential to delete any rules within the security group that reference other security groups or resources within the VPC, ensuring a clean and orderly removal process.</p>\n<p>By integrating AWS Glue DataBrew with a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC), you can securely access and process data from VPC's protected environment. This requires configuring security groups and VPC endpoints to control traffic flow and facilitate private connections. Proper network configuration is essential for maintaining secure and efficient workflows using DataBrew's visual interface for data cleaning and normalization tasks.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Detach the security group from all associated VPC resources and delete any security group rules within the VPC that reference the security group.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Reconfigure the VPC to utilize a new security group for DataBrew connectivity before deleting the old one</strong></span> is incorrect because reconfiguring to a new security group does not fulfill the requirement to properly detach and manage dependencies of the existing security group before its deletion.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Disable deletion protection on the security group in AWS Security Hub to allow its removal</strong></span> is incorrect. Deletion protection is a feature that applies only to AWS resources, such as EC2 instances and RDS databases, to prevent accidental deletion. Security groups, in general, don't have deletion protection and can be deleted once they are no longer associated with any resources.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Delete the Elastic Network Interface (ENI) associated with AWS Glue DataBrew before removing the security group</strong></span> is incorrect. Deleting an ENI is not enough to remove the associated security group. Additionally, deleting an ENI could disrupt network connectivity for services that rely on it, resulting in unintended consequences beyond the scope of securely managing security groups.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/what-is-amazon-vpc.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/what-is-amazon-vpc.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/databrew-with-vpc.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/databrew-with-vpc.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/working-with-security-groups.html#deleting-security-group\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/working-with-security-groups.html#deleting-security-group</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon VPC Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-vpc/\"><span>https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-vpc/</span></a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":34,"sourceNumber":46,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineering team is using AWS Glue for their ETL jobs as part of a larger data analytics solution. They are leveraging CloudWatch Logs Insights to analyze various types of logs from their AWS Glue jobs to optimize job performance and troubleshoot issues.</p>\n<p>Which of the following combinations of metrics and logs can be directly monitored and analyzed in CloudWatch Logs Insights?</p>","answers":["Apache Spark error logs, CPU usage, and Data Processing Unit (DPU) usage.","Apache Spark job progress bar, Apache Spark job logs, driver logs, and executor logs.","Quantum Processing unit (QPU), system logs, and CPU usage.","Apache Spark error logs, driver logs, and network usage."],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>AWS Glue provides real-time, continuous logging for AWS Glue jobs. You can view real-time <strong>Apache Spark job logs</strong> in Amazon CloudWatch, including <strong>driver logs, executor logs, and an Apache Spark job progress ba</strong>r. Viewing real-time logs provides you with a better perspective on the running job.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/c945b3744a9191051a267bf4eae45f84e18dc55619e0822dc226e9482c0317d1.jpg\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>When you start an AWS Glue job, it sends the real-time logging information to CloudWatch (every 5 seconds and before each executor termination) after the Spark application starts running. You can view the logs on the AWS Glue console or the CloudWatch console dashboard.</p>\n<p>The continuous logging feature includes the following capabilities:</p>\n<div>\n<p>- Continuous logging with a default filter to reduce high verbosity in the logs</p>\n<p>- Continuous logging with no filter</p>\n<p>- A custom script logger to log application-specific messages</p>\n<p>- A console progress bar to track the running status of the current AWS Glue job</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Apache Spark job progress bar, Apache Spark job logs, driver logs, and executor logs.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Apache Spark error logs, CPU usage, and Data Processing Unit (DPU) usage</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. CPU and Data Processing Unit (DPU) usage are metrics that cannot be directly monitored in CloudWatch Logs Insights. DPUs are a measure of computation power used by AWS Glue and do not produce logs that can be analyzed by CloudWatch Logs Insights.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Quantum Processing unit (QPU), system logs, and CPU usage</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. QPU refers to computing resources, specifically within the context of Amazon Braket, which is a service for exploring and experimenting with quantum computing. QPUs are not relevant to the log analysis of AWS Glue jobs. Moreover, CPU usage is monitored through CloudWatch Metrics rather than Logs Insights, which focuses on the analysis of log data.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Apache Spark error logs, driver logs, and network usage</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. While driver logs can be monitored and analyzed in CloudWatch Logs Insights, Apache Spark error logs and network usage can't be directly monitored in CloudWatch Logs Insights. Error logs provide information about errors that occurred during the execution of the Spark job, but they are not specific to AWS Glue and are not directly monitored by CloudWatch Logs Insights. Network usage is a system-level metric that is not directly related to the execution of the AWS Glue job.</p>\n</div>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-continuous-logging.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-continuous-logging.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/new-amazon-cloudwatch-log-class-to-cost-effectively-scale-your-aws-glue-workloads/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/new-amazon-cloudwatch-log-class-to-cost-effectively-scale-your-aws-glue-workloads/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":35,"sourceNumber":47,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineering team is enhancing the observability of an application deployed in an Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) cluster.</p>\n<p>The team wants to centralize the collection of low-level system metrics and logs from various microservices and enable near real-time visualization of these data for better insight and quicker response times.</p>\n<p>What solution will satisfy these requirements with the LEAST amount of operational overhead?</p>","answers":["Collect metrics and logs from the microservices using Amazon CloudWatch Container Insights. Configure a Data Firehose subscription filter to stream the logs to an Amazon OpenSearch Service cluster.","Configure an Amazon Kinesis Agent to channel the logs from the microservices to Kinesis Data Streams. Relay the logs to Amazon DynamoDB with AWS Lambda for storage and analysis.","Use Amazon CloudWatch Application Insights to aggregate metrics and logs from the microservices. Set up an AWS Lambda subscription filter to push the log data to an Amazon OpenSearch Service cluster.","Channel logs from microservices to Data Firehose, then relay the logs to OpenSearch Service for enhanced search and analysis."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><strong>Amazon CloudWatch Container Insights</strong> is a service that monitors, troubleshoots, and alarms on containerized applications and microservices. It collects metrics and logs from EKS, ECS, and Kubernetes clusters and provides automatic dashboards in the CloudWatch console for end-to-end visibility.</p>\n<p><strong>Data Firehose</strong> is a fully managed service that delivers real-time streaming data to destinations like S3, Redshift, and OpenSearch Service. A subscription filter in Firehose allows you to selectively filter logs from CloudWatch Logs and deliver them to the configured destination.</p>\n<p>The<strong> Amazon OpenSearch Service</strong> is a fully managed service that makes it easy to deploy, operate, and scale OpenSearch implementations. It provides enhanced security, availability, integration with other AWS services, and lower costs compared to managing your own OpenSearch cluster. Furthermore, it enables the visualization of log data through Kibana dashboards.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/c6040b1a6b71867578086fa089a8ed487d274fc595a11492ac7dcf100d89d5e1.jpg\" alt=\"Diagram of the given correct solution.\"></p>\n<p>The data engineering team can use Amazon CloudWatch Container Insights to collect metrics and logs from the microservices running in the various EKS clusters. They can configure a Firehose stream to transport the logs from Container Insights to Amazon OpenSearch Service. OpenSearch Service is a fully managed service that makes it easy to deploy, operate, and scale for log analytics. It allows indexing and visualizing the logs from Container Insights for near real-time search and analysis. This removes the operational overhead of managing the infrastructure. The team can use OpenSearch Dashboards or Kibana for visualization and Grafana for dashboards and alerts, providing a single pane of glass for monitoring across all their clusters and enabling improved troubleshooting capabilities.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <strong><span>Collect metrics and logs from the microservices using Amazon CloudWatch Container Insights. Configure a Data Firehose subscription filter to stream the logs to an Amazon OpenSearch Service cluster.</span></strong></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure an Amazon Kinesis Agent to channel the logs from the microservices to Kinesis Data Streams. Relay the logs to Amazon DynamoDB with AWS Lambda for storage and analysis&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect. This option does not provide an integrated solution for real-time visualization of query results. While it captures logs and uses AWS Lambda for processing, DynamoDB is primarily a NoSQL database suited for storage and not for the real-time visualization of logs and metrics.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use Amazon CloudWatch Application Insights to aggregate metrics and logs from the microservices. Set up an AWS Lambda subscription filter to push the log data to an Amazon OpenSearch Service cluster</strong></span> is incorrect. CloudWatch Application Insights is primarily designed for monitoring applications rather than being a direct solution for aggregating metrics and logs from microservices. It doesn't offer a built-in way to collect and visualize Kubernetes metrics or logs specifically. Furthermore, using AWS Lambda as an intermediary to deliver logs to Amazon OpenSearch introduces additional latency and complexity. AWS Lambda functions add an extra processing step, which can result in delays, especially under high throughput or when the function needs to scale.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Channel logs from microservices to Data Firehose, then relay the logs to OpenSearch Service for enhanced search and analysis</strong></span> is incorrect. While this option involves Amazon OpenSearch Service for search and analysis, it misses the integration with Amazon CloudWatch Container Insights, designed explicitly for monitoring containerized applications and provides essential metrics and logs crucial for observability.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/Subscriptions.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/Subscriptions.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/ContainerInsights.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/ContainerInsights.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out these Tutorials Dojo Cheat Sheets: </strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-cloudwatch/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-cloudwatch/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-opensearch-service/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-opensearch-service/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":36,"sourceNumber":48,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineer is looking to improve the efficiency of their data processing workflows on AWS, aiming to decrease the overall processing time. The solution involves executing multiple tasks concurrently within their AWS Step Functions managed workflow.</p>\n<p>Which AWS Step Functions state should the data engineer utilize to meet this requirement?</p>","answers":["<span><code>Fail</code></span>","<span><code>Parallel</code></span>","<span><code>Choice</code></span>","<span><code>Succeed</code></span>"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>In AWS Step Functions, the&nbsp;<code>Parallel</code>&nbsp;state is used to create parallel branches of execution in your state machine. AWS Step Functions automatically transitions to the next state after all branches of the&nbsp;<code>Parallel</code>&nbsp;state complete their tasks. This means that the&nbsp;<code>Parallel</code>&nbsp;state is the one to check to ensure all parallel processes have been completed successfully before moving on.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/a24820582364c76da0ffc04232ffbddb7bd6d1dbbc9c980c94e9fae1443d03d2.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>A&nbsp;<code>Parallel</code>&nbsp;state causes AWS Step Functions to execute each branch, starting with the state named in that branch's&nbsp;<code>StartAt</code>&nbsp;field, as concurrently as possible and wait until all branches terminate (reach a terminal state) before processing the&nbsp;<code>Parallel</code>&nbsp;state's&nbsp;<code>Next</code>&nbsp;field.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong><code>Parallel.</code></strong></span></p>\n<p><code><span><strong>Fail</strong></span></code>&nbsp;is incorrect because the&nbsp;<code>Fail</code>&nbsp;state in AWS Step Functions is used to terminate the state machine with a failure. It does not have the functionality to check the completion of parallel tasks.</p>\n<p><code><span><strong>Choice</strong></span></code>&nbsp;is incorrect because the&nbsp;<code>Choice</code>&nbsp;state simply allows for branching logic based on input data. While it can direct the workflow to different execution paths based on specified conditions, it does not support the concurrent execution of tasks.</p>\n<p><span><strong><code>Succeed</code></strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. This state is just&nbsp;used to terminate the execution of a state machine successfully. It does not have the functionality to check the completion of parallel tasks.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-states.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-states.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/amazon-states-language-parallel-state.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/amazon-states-language-parallel-state.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Step Functions Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":37,"sourceNumber":49,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company is using Amazon Redshift for its data warehousing needs. The company noticed that over 5 TB of its stored content is infrequently accessed. The company wants to optimize costs and storage space for this data.</p>\n<p>Which of the following options should the company choose to meet its requirements?</p>","answers":["<span>Use the <code>UNLOAD</code> command to export data from Amazon Redshift to Amazon S3 in Parquet format.</span>","<span>Use the <code>COPY</code> command to export data from Amazon Redshift to Amazon S3.</span>","Store infrequently accessed data in Amazon Redshift.","<span>Create an external table in Amazon Redshift Spectrum and load the data from Amazon Redshift using the <code>COPY</code> command.</span>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>The <code>UNLOAD</code> command in Amazon Redshift allows you to unload the result of a query to Amazon S3. This command is particularly useful for exporting data that is not frequently accessed, thereby saving storage space in Redshift and reducing costs.</p>\n<p>The <code>UNLOAD</code> command supports exporting data in various formats, including CSV, JSON, and Parquet. Parquet is an efficient open columnar storage format for analytics. It is up to 2x faster to unload and consumes up to 6x less storage in Amazon S3 compared to text formats. This makes Parquet a good choice for storing large volumes of infrequently accessed data.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/a9068ce28f6c24a15d6677532f03e568996facab16c2f91cc7cb828aa7c3fa4c.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Amazon S3 is a cost-effective storage service. The cost of storing data in S3 depends on the amount of data stored, the duration of storage, and the storage class. By exporting infrequently accessed data from Redshift to S3, the company can optimize its storage costs.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<span><strong> Use the <code>UNLOAD</code> command to export data from Amazon Redshift to Amazon S3 in Parquet format.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Use the <code>COPY</code> command to export data from Amazon Redshift to Amazon S3 </span></strong>is incorrect because the <code>COPY</code> command is used to load data into Amazon Redshift from data files from an Amazon DynamoDB table. It’s not used to export data from Amazon Redshift.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Store infrequently accessed data in Amazon Redshift </strong></span>is incorrect because storing infrequently accessed data in Amazon Redshift can be expensive as Redshift is designed for high-performance analysis and not for storing infrequently accessed data.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create an external table in Amazon Redshift Spectrum and load the data from Amazon Redshift using the <code>COPY</code> command </strong></span>is incorrect. Redshift Spectrum is simply a feature that allows you to query data directly in S3 using Redshift SQL queries without having to load the data into Redshift. You would typically use Redshift Spectrum to query data already in S3. Once the data is in S3, you can create external tables that reference this data. Redshift Spectrum does not have its own built-in storage; rather, it serves as a bridge for querying external data.</p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_UNLOAD.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_UNLOAD.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":38,"sourceNumber":51,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p><span>A company utilizes Amazon Redshift for its primary data warehousing needs tied to an e-commerce application. Recently, they added a new SQL report for their sales data, which needs to compare specific columns from this year's sales against historical data. Data older than one year is stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. This new report has resulted in slower performance and increased query times. The company needs a solution to enhance system performance and speed up these queries.</span></p>\n<p><span>What is the MOST efficient solution to meet these requirements?</span></p>","answers":["<span>Run the <code>VACUUM DELETE</code> command in Redshift to clean up and optimize the data before running the report. Afterward, execute the <code>SQL COPY</code> command to load the data from Amazon S3 into Redshift. Adjust the report to pull data from the latest and newly imported tables.</span>","<span>Run a <code>SQL JOIN</code> operation with Amazon Redshift Spectrum to merge the most recent data with the S3 external table data into a new table. Adjust the report to access data from this newly formed table.</span>","<span>Use the report's SQL statement to retrieve data directly from Amazon S3. Save these results in a Redshift materialized view. Adjust the report to refresh using <code>SQL REFRESH</code> and then extract data from the materialized view.</span>","<span>Run the <code>SQL UNLOAD</code> command to move data from the sales table to a new external table in Amazon S3. Adjust the report to utilize Amazon Redshift Spectrum for querying the newly created table and the existing tables in Amazon S3.</span>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Amazon Redshift Spectrum allows you to efficiently query and retrieve structured and semistructured data from files in Amazon S3 without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables. Redshift Spectrum queries employ massive parallelism to run very fast against large datasets. Much of the processing occurs in the Redshift Spectrum layer, and most of the data remains in Amazon S3. Like Amazon Athena, Redshift Spectrum is serverless, and there’s nothing to provision or manage. You just pay for the resources you consume for the duration of your Redshift Spectrum query. Like Amazon Redshift itself, you get the benefits of a sophisticated query optimizer, fast access to data on local disks, and standard SQL.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/16f0325ad19dfed538cd42a4ec50e189cca36f8f333835d5878f4e59acabd972.gif\"></p>\n<p>Amazon Redshift offers several features to enhance query performance, such as result caching, concurrency scaling, and materialized views. Materialized views in Redshift provide a powerful way to optimize query performance for repeated and predictable query workloads. They precompute and store the results of a query, which improves the speed of data retrieval for complex queries that are run frequently. This makes them particularly useful for scenarios like the one described, where data from historical records stored in Amazon S3 is regularly queried alongside newer data. Redshift can use materialized views to serve query results from precomputed data, reducing the compute resources required and accelerating query performance, thus enabling near real-time analytics on large datasets.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Use the report's SQL statement to retrieve data directly from Amazon S3. Save these results in a Redshift materialized view. Adjust the report to refresh using <code>SQL REFRESH</code> and then extract data from the materialized view.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Run the <code>VACUUM DELETE</code> command in Redshift to clean up and optimize the data before running the report. Afterward, execute the <code>SQL COPY</code> command to load the data from Amazon S3 into Redshift. Adjust the report to pull data from the latest and newly imported tables</strong></span> is incorrect. Although this option suggests a good practice, optimizing data using <code>VACUUM DELETE</code>, copying data from Amazon S3 using the SQL COPY command is resource-extensive and may not be the most efficient approach, especially when the data is already in Amazon S3. Always remember that Amazon Redshift already has a suite of features that allows it to execute specific operations for data hosted in Amazon S3.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Run a <code>SQL JOIN</code> operation with Amazon Redshift Spectrum to merge the most recent data with the S3 external table data into a new table. Adjust the report to access data from this newly formed table</span></strong> is incorrect. While Redshift Spectrum effectively allows for querying data in S3 without moving it into Redshift, this approach relies heavily on the continuous execution of SQL JOINs across Redshift and S3. This approach can introduce significant query overhead, especially when the datasets or queries are complex because it must dynamically handle large volumes of data across the two services, which can cause delays in the query execution time.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Run the <code>SQL UNLOAD</code> command to move data from the sales table to a new external table in Amazon S3. Adjust the report to utilize Amazon Redshift Spectrum for querying the newly created table and the existing tables in Amazon S3</strong></span> is incorrect. This involves loading and unloading data in and out of Amazon Redshift and Amazon S3, which consumes many unnecessary resources. If you already host your data in Amazon S3, it is best to query it directly from Amazon Redshift instead of moving data between Redshift and S3. Additionally, this option introduces complexity with multiple tables and data transfer steps.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-create-sql-command.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-create-sql-command.html</a></p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":39,"sourceNumber":52,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A large-scale data analytics company is migrating their big data platform to Amazon Web Services (AWS). They have chosen to use Amazon Elastic MapReduce (EMR) for processing large datasets and Amazon Aurora as a Hive metastore.</p>\n<p>The company’s data engineers need to configure Amazon EMR to use Amazon Aurora as a Hive metastore. This setup will allow the metadata to persist even after the EMR cluster is terminated, enabling cost savings and improved query performance.</p>\n<p>Which of the following steps need to be taken? (SELECT THREE.)</p>","answers":["Create a MySQL or PostgreSQL Aurora  database.","Modify security groups to allow JDBC connections.","Set JDBC configuration values in hive-site.xml.","Configure the AWS Glue Data Catalog.","Set up cross-account access for EMR Serverless and AWS Glue Data Catalog.","Ensure that you do not write to the same metastore table concurrently."],"correct":[0,1,2],"explanation":"<p>Amazon EMR uses an external database to store metadata about the tables, schema, and other Hive data structures. The company’s data engineers would need to create an Amazon Aurora database (either MySQL or PostgreSQL) that will serve as the Hive metastore. This is a crucial step because the Hive metastore is a central repository of Hive metadata. It persists metadata for Hive tables and partitions in a relational database, and provides high availability, and ease of use.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/ea518a13e89bb402f9570db7c72a5b59b14a2bdc4bf585a8d0b3d560f890e110.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Hive uses JDBC to connect to the metastore database. The data engineers would need to modify the security groups of both the Amazon EMR cluster and the Amazon Aurora database to allow these connections. This is important because the security group acts like a virtual firewall that controls the traffic for one or more instances. When you launch an instance, you can associate one or more security groups with the instance. You add rules to each security group that allow traffic to or from its associated instances.</p>\n<p>Set JDBC configuration values in hive-site.xml. This involves creating a configuration file called&nbsp;<code>hiveConfiguration.json</code>&nbsp;containing edits to&nbsp;<code>hive-site.xml</code>. This file specifies the JDBC connection parameters that Hive uses to connect to the metastore database. The data engineers would need to set the appropriate JDBC configuration values in this file to point to the Amazon Aurora database. This is necessary because the&nbsp;<code>hive-site.xml</code>&nbsp;file is where Hive reads the configuration for the metastore connection, among other settings. The JDBC connection parameters that Hive uses to connect to the metastore database are defined in this file.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span><strong>- Create a MySQL or PostgreSQL Aurora database</strong>.</span></p>\n<p><span><strong>- Modify security groups to allow JDBC connections</strong>.</span></p>\n<p><span><strong>- Set JDBC configuration values in hive-site.xml</strong>.</span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Configure the AWS Glue Data Catalog</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect because it is not relevant when using Amazon Aurora as the Hive metastore. AWS Glue Data Catalog is an alternative to using an external Hive metastore, but it’s not used in conjunction with Aurora for this purpose.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Set up cross-account access for EMR Serverless and AWS Glue Data Catalog&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect because it is also not relevant when using Amazon Aurora as the Hive metastore. This would be necessary if you were using the AWS Glue Data Catalog and needed to access it from a different AWS account, but it’s not applicable in this scenario.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Ensure that you do not write to the same metastore table concurrently</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because this is just a cautionary step to avoid data conflicts when sharing metastore information between two clusters. While it’s good practice to avoid concurrent writes, it’s not a step in the process of configuring Amazon EMR to use Amazon Aurora as a Hive metastore. It’s more of a usage guideline after the setup is complete. The data engineers would need to be aware of this guideline when using the Hive metastore, but it’s not a step they would need to take to set up the metastore.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-hive-metastore-external.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-hive-metastore-external.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-metastore-external-hive.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-metastore-external-hive.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/migrate-and-deploy-your-apache-hive-metastore-on-amazon-emr/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/migrate-and-deploy-your-apache-hive-metastore-on-amazon-emr/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out these Amazon Aurora and Amazon EMR Cheat Sheets:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-aurora/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-aurora/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":40,"sourceNumber":53,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company is planning to migrate its centralized metadata store for Apache Hive to AWS. The metadata store exposes S3 objects as tables that can be used by all data analytics teams running their own Amazon EMR clusters. Each team has its own metadata for running different SQL queries using Hive.</p>\n<p>Which solution would be the most suitable for this task?</p>","answers":["Use AWS Glue Data Catalog as the external metastore for Hive.","Use EMR File System (EMRFS) as the external metastore for Hive.","Use EMR notebook to extract metadata from your Hive metastore, then load the metadata and update the AWS Glue Data Catalog.","Use Amazon Athena to query data directly from S3 using standard SQL."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Glue Data Catalog</strong>&nbsp;is a fully-managed service that serves as a centralized metadata repository. It can be used as a drop-in replacement for the Apache Hive Metastore, providing a unified metadata repository across various services. This is crucial when you have multiple data analytics teams each running their own Amazon EMR clusters and using their own metadata for running different SQL queries using Hive.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/f1753e710dc6fc149972429ded3dfa191e063d803326c81f4d523700b1bf97f7.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>One of the key features of AWS Glue Data Catalog is its ability to crawl data sources to discover schemas and populate your Catalog with new and modified table and partition definitions. This means that it can automatically keep the metadata up-to-date, reducing the need for manual management.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, AWS Glue Data Catalog maintains schema versioning. This means that it keeps track of all changes to the metadata, allowing you to easily revert to a previous version if necessary.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <strong><span>Use AWS Glue Data Catalog as the external metastore for Hive.</span></strong></p>\n<p>The option that says: <strong><span>Use EMR File System (EMRFS) as the external metastore for Hive</span></strong>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect&nbsp;because EMRFS is an Amazon S3-compatible file system that you can use with Amazon EMR. It is not designed to serve as an external metastore for Hive.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use EMR notebook to extract metadata from your Hive metastore, then load the metadata and update the AWS Glue Data Catalog</strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect&nbsp;because EMR notebooks are primarily used for creating and sharing documents that contain live code, equations, visualizations, and narrative text, not for extracting metadata from Hive metastore.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>U</strong><strong><span>se</span> Amazon Athena to query data directly from S3 using standard SQL</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because Amazon Athena allows you to query data directly from S3 using standard SQL, bypassing the need for a traditional data warehouse. However, this does not provide a centralized metadata store solution.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/migrate-and-deploy-your-apache-hive-metastore-on-amazon-emr/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/migrate-and-deploy-your-apache-hive-metastore-on-amazon-emr/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/serverless-etl-aws-glue/aws-glue-data-catalog.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/serverless-etl-aws-glue/aws-glue-data-catalog.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":41,"sourceNumber":54,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A large e-commerce company has an Amazon Redshift data warehouse that contains about 300 TB of consumer data. The Redshift cluster is updated every 6 hours by importing newly gathered consumer activity data. Read-only queries are executed throughout the day to generate product patterns used to curate the company’s website homepage. However, there is a particular long-running query that runs for more than two hours every hour, causing other short-running queries to get queued up or take a longer time to execute.</p>\n<p>Which of the following would be the most cost-effective solution to optimize query execution?</p>","answers":["Enable auto Concurrency Scaling mode and set up a distinct Workload Manager (WLM) queue for the long-running query.","Expand the number of nodes in the Redshift cluster to accommodate the increased load.","Activate Short Query Acceleration (SQA) to give priority to short-running queries.","Adjust the WLM settings to terminate the long-running query if it exceeds two hours of execution time."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Amazon Redshift’s Concurrency Scaling feature allows you to automatically add additional cluster capacity to handle an increase in concurrent queries. This feature is designed to support thousands of concurrent users and concurrent queries with consistently fast query performance. When you turn on concurrency scaling, Amazon Redshift automatically adds additional cluster capacity to process an increase in both read and write queries. This means that users see the most current data, whether the queries run on the main cluster or a concurrency-scaling cluster.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/14afa3011ee91dd2ce1f9cb018646ce3e67aeac41d0356f49a7ee42b3cfee256.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>By configuring a separate WLM queue for the long-running query, you can ensure that this query does not block other queries from executing. Amazon Redshift workload management (WLM) enables users to flexibly manage priorities within workloads so that short, fast-running queries won’t get stuck in queues behind long-running queries. When you run a query, WLM assigns the query to a queue according to the user’s user group or by matching a query group that is listed in the queue configuration with a query group label that the user sets at runtime.</p>\n<p>This approach is cost-effective because you are charged for concurrency-scaling clusters only for the time they’re actively running queries.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Enable auto Concurrency Scaling mode and set up a distinct Workload Manager (WLM) queue for the long-running query.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Expand the number of nodes in the Redshift cluster to accommodate the increased load</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect. Increasing the number of nodes can improve performance by providing more resources to handle the load. However, this approach might not be the most cost-effective solution, as it involves additional costs for the extra nodes. It’s also worth noting that simply adding more nodes may not solve the problem if the issue is related to query optimization or workload management.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Activate Short Query Acceleration (SQA) to give priority to short-running queries</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect. While SQA might improve the execution time of other, shorter queries, it doesn’t directly address the issue with the long-running query. The long-running query would still take the same amount of time to execute and could still cause performance issues when it’s running.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Adjust the WLM settings to terminate the long-running query if it exceeds two hours of execution time</span>&nbsp;</strong>is incorrect. While terminating long-running queries can free up resources for other queries, it might not be the best approach if the long-running query is critical for your business operations. It’s also important to note that terminating a query does not address the root cause of why the query is taking a long time to run. A better approach might be to optimize the query or adjust the workload management settings to better handle long-running queries.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/concurrency-scaling-queues.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/concurrency-scaling-queues.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/cm-c-implementing-workload-management.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/cm-c-implementing-workload-management.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/scale-read-and-write-workloads-with-amazon-redshift/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/scale-read-and-write-workloads-with-amazon-redshift/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":42,"sourceNumber":55,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineer is working on a machine learning project that requires real-time feature access for model predictions and batch feature access for training models. The engineer decides to use Amazon SageMaker Feature Store for managing the features. The broader application also uses Amazon Cognito to manage user authentication for applications that consume the model's predictions.</p>\n<p>Which mode of SageMaker Feature Store should the engineer choose to meet the requirements of both real-time feature access and batch feature access?</p>","answers":["Online","Offline","Online and Offline","Batch"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>In Feature Store, features are stored in a collection called a&nbsp;<em>feature group</em>.&nbsp;You can visualize a feature group as a table in which each column is a feature, with a unique identifier for each row. In principle, a feature group is composed of features and values specific to each feature. A&nbsp;<code>Record</code>&nbsp;is a collection of values for features that correspond to a unique&nbsp;<code>RecordIdentifier</code>. Altogether, a&nbsp;<code>FeatureGroup</code>&nbsp;is a group of features defined in your&nbsp;<code>FeatureStore</code>&nbsp;to describe a&nbsp;<code>Record</code>.</p>\n<p>You can use Feature Store in&nbsp;the following modes:</p>\n<div>\n<p><strong>Online</strong>&nbsp;– In online mode, features are read with low latency (milliseconds) reads and used for high throughput predictions. This mode requires a feature group to be stored in an online store.</p>\n<p><strong>Offline</strong>&nbsp;– In offline mode, large streams of data are&nbsp;fed to an offline store, which can be used for training and batch inference. This mode requires a feature group to be stored in an offline store. The offline store uses your S3 bucket for storage and can also fetch data using Athena queries.</p>\n<p><strong>Online and Offline</strong>&nbsp;– This includes both online and offline modes.</p>\n</div>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/8922c56fadcb3c2a7a23bd8e629a8753576605351f8675c221bb63d0a872bf15.png\" alt=\"SageMaker Feature Store workflow for online inference and offline model training\"></p>\n<p>You can ingest data into feature groups in Feature Store in two ways: streaming or in batches. When you ingest data through streaming, a collection of records are pushed to the Feature Store by calling a synchronous&nbsp;<code>PutRecord</code>&nbsp;API call. This API enables you to maintain the latest feature values in the Feature Store and to push new feature values as soon as an update is detected.</p>\n<p>Alternatively, Feature Store can process and ingest data in batches. You can author features using Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler, create feature groups in Feature Store, and ingest features in batches using a SageMaker Processing job with a notebook exported from Data Wrangler. This mode allows for batch ingestion into the offline store. It also supports ingestion into the online store if the feature group is configured for both online and offline use.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Online and Offline.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>Online</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because it only provides low-latency real-time access to features, which is suitable for model predictions. However, it does not support batch feature access, which is needed for training models.</p>\n<p><span><strong>Offline</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because it only supports batch feature access, which is suitable for training models. However, it does not provide real-time feature access, which is needed for model predictions.</p>\n<p><span><strong>Batch</strong></span> is incorrect because there is no “Batch” mode in the Amazon SageMaker Feature Store. The Offline store provides batch feature access, but it does not provide real-time feature access.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/feature-store.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/feature-store.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/simplify-iterative-machine-learning-model-development-by-adding-features-to-existing-feature-groups-in-amazon-sagemaker-feature-store/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/simplify-iterative-machine-learning-model-development-by-adding-features-to-existing-feature-groups-in-amazon-sagemaker-feature-store/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon SageMaker AI Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":43,"sourceNumber":56,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineer uses Amazon Athena to run nested <code>SELECT</code> queries for joining multiple tables. The data engineer wants to run additional queries on these results but finds the process challenging and time-consuming.</p>\n<p>Which of the following would simplify the querying process for the team?</p>","answers":["Create separate views in Amazon Athena for each table involved in the queries.","Enable the Query Result Reuse feature in Amazon Athena.","<span>Create a view in Amazon Athena for each <code>SELECT</code> query.</span>","Use the Athena workgroup settings for query execution management"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>In Amazon Athena, a view is a logical, not a physical table. It’s essentially a saved query that you can reference, just like a table. This is particularly useful when dealing with complex queries. In the given scenario, the data engineer has already prepared complex <code>SELECT</code>&nbsp;queries that join multiple tables for data aggregation. These queries are likely intricate and time-consuming to write and execute. By creating a view for each of these&nbsp;<code>SELECT</code>&nbsp;queries, the team can save the definitions of these complex queries. This means that they don’t have to write the entire query each time they want to use it; they can simply reference the view. This significantly simplifies the querying process.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/ddcd68f6cb8517357b13f9161dfe78a990448b33d2ae75702f939529b03a0add.png\"></p>\n<p>Furthermore, the team can run further queries against these views. In this context, the views act as virtual tables or composed queries on the underlying data. This is a more efficient approach as it reduces the time and computational resources required to run complex queries repeatedly. It also allows the team to focus on data analysis rather than data management.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Create a view in Amazon Athena for each <code>SELECT</code> query.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Enable the Query Result Reuse feature in Amazon Athena</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. This feature would only&nbsp;speed up queries by caching the results of previous queries. It won't inherently simplify the process of writing or managing complex queries.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create separate views in Amazon Athena for each table involved in the queries</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect.&nbsp;This method would be more helpful if simplifying access to individual tables is the goal. However, the challenge is to simplify running queries against the results of a single complex query. This approach would still require complex joins and logic to be applied every time the data is queried.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use the Athena workgroup settings for query execution management</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Workgroup is just a way for you to control query execution and access, set data usage limits, and configure query result locations. It won't help in simplifying.&nbsp;While it's an essential feature for managing Athena usage at scale, it doesn't directly simplify the writing or structuring of complex queries.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/views.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/views.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/views.html#creating-views\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/views.html#creating-views</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":44,"sourceNumber":57,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A data engineering team uses a custom-built application to generate sales reports for a company. The data is hosted in Amazon Redshift, and the application logic executes 30 SQL statements sequentially when generating the report. The team wants to simplify the operation and improve the network traffic between the application and the Redshift database.</p>\n<p>Which of the following would be the MOST effective solution?</p>","answers":["Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to run the SQL statements in parallel.","Use Amazon Redshift User-defined functions to encapsulate the SQL statements.","Use Amazon Redshift Stored Procedures to encapsulate the SQL statements.","Use Amazon Redshift ML to predict the results of the SQL statements."],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>In Amazon Redshift, stored procedures serve as a mechanism to encapsulate logic for various operations, such as data transformation and validation, as well as business-specific logic. This encapsulation allows for a more streamlined and efficient process, particularly when dealing with multiple SQL statements. By bundling these statements into a single stored procedure, the number of round trips between the custom-built application and the database can be significantly reduced, thereby simplifying the operation and enhancing network traffic efficiency.</p>\n<p>Stored procedures are stored directly in the database, making them accessible to any user with the appropriate privileges. They offer a level of flexibility not found in user-defined functions (UDFs), as they can incorporate not only SELECT queries but also data definition language (DDL) and data manipulation language (DML). Unlike UDFs, stored procedures do not necessarily need to return a value.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/34891ff4086fcab102cbc094d0f0206cd845ce68e259b3da675752eba82f3ed2.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Stored procedures in Amazon Redshift are used to encapsulate logic for data transformation, data validation, and business-specific logic. By combining multiple SQL steps into a stored procedure, you can reduce round trips between your custom-built application and the database. This would simplify the operation and improve the network traffic between the custom-built application and Redshift.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<strong>Use Amazon Redshift Stored Procedures to encapsulate the SQL statements.</strong></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to run the SQL statements in parallel</strong></span> is incorrect because Amazon Redshift Spectrum is only useful when querying data directly against Amazon S3. Moreover, the idea of running SQL statements in parallel is more about optimizing query performance across distributed data rather than simplifying operational processes or reducing the application-database interaction overhead.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use Amazon Redshift User-defined functions to encapsulate the SQL statements</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. UDFs are typically used to encapsulate frequently used calculations or transformations within SQL queries, not for managing a sequence of data retrieval tasks. UDFs execute within the context of a single SQL query and cannot manage the flow control of multiple sequential operations.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use Amazon Redshift ML to predict the results of the SQL statements</strong></span> is incorrect because Amazon Redshift ML is simply a feature for training and applying machine learning models using familiar SQL commands. Predicting the outcome of a sequence of SQL statements isn't within its scope. Additionally,&nbsp;when generating reports with SQL queries, the aim is to absolute values from the actual database data, not to predict outcomes as with machine learning models.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/stored-procedure-create.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/stored-procedure-create.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/stored-procedure-enhancements-in-amazon-redshift/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/stored-procedure-enhancements-in-amazon-redshift/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":45,"sourceNumber":58,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company needs to periodically assess air quality changes in a particular city. The company recently launched a serverless data pipeline on AWS that feeds data into an Amazon QuickSight dashboard.</p>\n<p>After a month, the data engineering team noticed that the dashboard data was obsolete and did not refresh due to the data pipeline's failure to execute every week. To solve this issue, the team must put in place a system to orchestrate the data workflow properly.</p>\n<p>Which solution can fulfill these requirements with the LEAST operational effort?</p>","answers":["Utilize AWS Step Functions for workflow orchestration and Amazon EventBridge for the weekly scheduling of tasks.","Implement Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (MWAA) for both orchestration and weekly task scheduling.","Configure Amazon S3 Events to trigger an AWS Lambda function, which in turn schedules an AWS Glue job weekly.","Use AWS CodePipeline for orchestration tasks, complemented by Amazon EventBridge for the pipeline's weekly scheduling."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><strong>AWS Step Functions</strong> serves as a serverless function orchestrator, enabling the design and execution of complex workflows across AWS services. It simplifies the automation of data pipelines by managing tasks, ensuring they execute in order, handling errors, and retrying as needed. This service is instrumental in orchestrating tasks that transform, analyze, and store data, providing visibility and control over each execution step.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/3a3ef8e5d1b2305384b0b83557f8e96d33264e42873a2ba91f0794bbd8bfd0b8.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p><strong>Amazon EventBridge</strong> offers a serverless event bus that connects applications with data from various sources. It schedules and triggers workflows automatically, such as those orchestrated by AWS Step Functions, based on a predefined schedule using cron or rate expressions. EventBridge facilitates the automation of the data pipeline's execution on a weekly basis without the need for manual intervention, aligning with the requirement for operational simplicity.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<span><strong>&nbsp;Utilize AWS Step Functions for workflow orchestration and Amazon EventBridge for the weekly scheduling of tasks.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Implement Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (MWAA) for both orchestration and weekly task scheduling&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect&nbsp;because&nbsp;it simply introduces a higher operational and maintenance overhead compared to the serverless solutions provided by AWS Step Functions and Amazon EventBridge, making it less ideal for the specified requirements.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Configure Amazon S3 Events to trigger an AWS Lambda function, which in turn schedules an AWS Glue job weekly&nbsp;</span></strong>is incorrect&nbsp;because it only complicates the&nbsp;workflow with unnecessary intermediaries and manual setup steps, deviating from the goal of operational simplicity and automation. Using a combination of AWS Step Functions and Amazon EventBridge should suffice.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use AWS CodePipeline for orchestration tasks, complemented by Amazon EventBridge for the pipeline's weekly scheduling&nbsp;</strong></span>is incorrect because the AWS CodePipeline service is primarily tailored more towards continuous integration and delivery rather than data pipeline orchestration. This makes it less suitable for the task compared to the more tailored and integrated capabilities of AWS Step Functions and EventBridge for data workflows.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\"> https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-what-is.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-what-is.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/cw-events.html#cw-events-execution-started\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/cw-events.html#cw-events-execution-started</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this AWS Step Functions Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":46,"sourceNumber":60,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A tech startup uses various AWS services to collect, process, and store user activity data for its mobile payment service. Its data engineering team is tasked with designing a cost-effective solution utilizing Amazon S3, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon QuickSight.</p>\n<p>The data from the last 60 days must be fetched from the DynamoDB table and displayed on a custom QuickSight dashboard for monthly analysis. The older user activity data must be archived in a data lake for 2 years before being purged. During this period, the startup is expected to occasionally query the data using Amazon Athena.</p>\n<p>Which combination of steps provides the MOST cost-effective solution? (Select THREE.)</p>","answers":["Use the Time to Live (TTL) feature to automatically delete items older than 60 days from the DynamoDB table.","Set up an AWS Glue job to extract the last 60 days of data from the DynamoDB table and load it into Amazon QuickSight for visualization.","Configure an Amazon S3 Lifecycle policy to transition user activity data older than 60 days to the S3 Infrequent Access (S3-IA) storage class and then permanently delete it after 2 years.","Archive older user activity data using Amazon Redshift Serverless instead of Amazon S3. Fetch data using the Amazon Athena SQL query editor.","Store all user activity data indefinitely in the DynamoDB table. Configure the table to use the DynamoDB Standard-Infrequent Access (Standard-IA) table class.","Use Amazon EMR Serverless to query the data in the S3 data lake. Set the S3 Lifecycle policy to transition the older user activity data to the S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class."],"correct":[0,1,2],"explanation":"<p>Amazon DynamoDB TTL is a feature that allows you to define a per-item timestamp to determine when an item is no longer needed. Once the timestamp expires, the item is marked for deletion from your table. This is a cost-effective way to reduce storage usage by deleting items that are no longer needed. In this scenario, setting a TTL of 60 days for items in the DynamoDB table would ensure that only the most recent 60 days of data are stored in the table.</p>\n<p>AWS Glue is a fully managed ETL (extract, transform, and load) service that makes it easy to prepare and load your data for analytics. You can create and run an ETL job with a few clicks in the AWS Management Console. AWS Glue generates the ETL code that is customizable, reusable, and portable. Once your ETL job is ready, you can schedule it to run on AWS Glue’s fully managed, scale-out Apache Spark environment. AWS Glue provides a console and API operations to set up and manage your extract, transform, and load (ETL) workload. It also provides a Jupyter Notebook server you can use to develop your ETL scripts interactively.<br>\n<img src=\"exams/assets/2c4b89ab3c5ec9ea26b03e1acd076147c35de53b57956d6eb1bd6adf737f777e.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>Amazon S3 offers storage management features that let you organize your data and configure finely-tuned access controls to meet specific business, organizational, and compliance requirements. Amazon S3 is designed for 99.999999999% (11 9’s) of durability and stores data for millions of applications for companies worldwide. S3 Lifecycle policies make transitioning data from S3 to Archive or deleting old data versions simple and cost-effective.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answers are:</p>\n<p><span><strong>- Use the Time to Live (TTL) feature to automatically delete items older than 60 days from the DynamoDB table.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>- Set up an AWS Glue job to extract the last 60 days of data from the DynamoDB table and load it into Amazon QuickSight for visualization.</strong></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>- Configure an Amazon S3 Lifecycle policy to transition user activity data older than 60 days to the S3 Infrequent Access (S3-IA) storage class and then permanently delete it after 2 years.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:<span><strong> Archive older user activity data using Amazon Redshift Serverless instead of Amazon S3. Fetch data using the Amazon Athena SQL query editor</strong></span> is incorrect because Amazon Redshift is primarily a data warehousing solution designed for online analytic processing (OLAP) and not as a data lake. The scenario explicitly mentions that the older user activity data must be archived in a data lake and not in a data warehouse. Amazon S3 is cheaper than Redshift for long-term data storage, making it a more cost-effective choice for archiving older user activity data.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Store all user activity data indefinitely in the DynamoDB table. Configure the table to use the DynamoDB Standard-Infrequent Access (Standard-IA) table class</strong></span> is incorrect because DynamoDB charges you for read and write capacity, as well as the amount of data stored; thus, storing data indefinitely will effectively increase the operating cost of the solution. Using a Standard-Infrequent Access (Standard-IA) table class is more suitable for data that is meant to be stored for a long time and not for items that are meant to expire in a period of 60 days. It’s more cost-effective to move older data to cheaper storage solutions like S3 and enable the DynamoDB table's TTL feature for automatic data deletion.</p>\n<p>The option that says:<span><strong> Use Amazon EMR Serverless to query the data in the S3 data lake. Set the S3 Lifecycle policy to transition the older user activity data to the S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class</strong></span> is incorrect. Although Amazon EMR can be used for querying data in an S3 data lake, the scenario specifies that the startup intends to use Amazon Athena for this purpose; therefore, using EMR would be unnecessary. While storing the data in S3 Glacier Deep Archive provides the cheapest storage option, it would entail a significant operational overhead since you have to restore the Glacier objects before you can access them.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TTL.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TTL.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":47,"sourceNumber":61,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A Data Engineer at a large e-commerce company is managing a DynamoDB table that stores product data from various categories. The partition key currently in use is <code>CATEGORY_NAME</code>. However, this has led to some partitions receiving more requests than others, causing imbalanced I/O and potential throttling.</p>\n<p>Which of the following strategies should the engineer implement to balance the throughput and prevent “hot” partitions?</p>","answers":["<span>Create a local secondary index (LSI) on <code>PRODUCT_ID</code> in a new DynamoDB database.</span>","<span>Create a new DynamoDB table called <code>DEPARTMENT_ID</code> with a more general partition key.</span>","<span>Create a new DynamoDB table with a Global Secondary Index (GSI) on <code>PRODUCT_NAME</code>.</span>","<span>Create a new DynamoDB table using a more specific partition key, for instance, <code>PRODUCT_ID</code>.</span>"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>In Amazon DynamoDB, data is distributed across multiple partitions based on the partition key. Each partition can handle a certain amount of read and write capacity. If the workload is not evenly distributed across partitions, some partitions (known as “hot” partitions) may receive a higher volume of traffic than others, leading to throttling and inefficient use of provisioned capacity.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/d4f667b3958aeea2db1a18c0c7131cc67a48d33855cb255590cf58a99bc1cb9c.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>The partition key currently in &nbsp;use is&nbsp;<code>CATEGORY_NAME</code>. However, if the data within a category is heavily accessed, it can lead to imbalanced I/O and potential throttling. This is because all items with the same&nbsp;<code>CATEGORY_NAME</code> are stored in the same partition, and if that category is popular, it can lead to a “hot” partition.</p>\n<p>The solution is to create a new DynamoDB table using a more specific partition key, such as&nbsp;<code>PRODUCT_ID</code>.&nbsp;<code>PRODUCT_ID</code>&nbsp;is likely to have a higher cardinality (i.e., more unique values) than&nbsp;<code>CATEGORY_NAME</code>, which means the data will be spread across more partitions. This results in a more even distribution of data and requests, reducing the likelihood of “hot” partitions.</p>\n<p>By using&nbsp;<code>PRODUCT_ID</code> as the partition key, each product would have its own partition. This means that even if one product is very popular, the high traffic will be isolated to that product’s partition and will not impact the performance of other partitions. This strategy ensures efficient use of your provisioned I/O capacity and prevents throttling.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Create a new DynamoDB table using a more specific partition key, for instance,&nbsp;<code>PRODUCT_ID</code>.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create a local secondary index (LSI) on&nbsp;<code>PRODUCT_ID</code> in a new DynamoDB database</strong>&nbsp;</span>is incorrect.&nbsp;An LSI does not change the underlying partition key or the distribution of data across partitions. It merely provides an alternative way of querying&nbsp;by allowing you to create an index on non-key attributes and query the data based on those attribute.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create a new DynamoDB table called&nbsp;<code>DEPARTMENT_ID</code> with a more general partition key</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. The issue with this approach is that departments could still have an imbalanced number of products, leading to potential \"hot\" partitions. For example, some departments might have a large number of products, while others might have fewer products. This could result in a similar situation as the current&nbsp;<code>CATEGORY_NAME</code> partition key, where certain partitions receive a disproportionate amount of requests</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create a new DynamoDB table with a Global Secondary Index (GSI) on&nbsp;<code>PRODUCT_NAME</code></strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect because GSIs simply allow you to create a new view of the data where you can use a different partition key and sort key. However, creating a GSI on&nbsp;<code>PRODUCT_NAME</code> would not address the issue of imbalanced I/O on the main table. GSIs are best used for improving the flexibility of queries, not for balancing throughput on the main table.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/bp-partition-key-uniform-load.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/bp-partition-key-uniform-load.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/bp-partition-key-design.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/bp-partition-key-design.html</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon DynamoDb Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":48,"sourceNumber":62,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A company uses an Amazon OpenSearch Service cluster for log analytics. Due to regulatory compliance, the company must store a large amount of index data for an indefinite period of time. The data is accessed infrequently, but when accessed, it needs to be available immediately. The company also wants to minimize costs as much as possible.</p>\n<p>Which solution would meet the requirements?</p>","answers":["Store the index in an OR1 storage","Add UltraWarm nodes on the cluster and move the index to it.","Move the index in a Cold storage.","Use Index State Management (ISM) to schedule the deletion of the index data."],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>UltraWarm nodes in Amazon OpenSearch Service utilize Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) along with associated caching solutions to enhance performance. UltraWarm provides a significantly lower cost per GiB for read-only data that is queried less frequently and doesn’t require the same performance level as hot storage. Although data in UltraWarm can’t be modified, it can be moved to the hot storage tier for modifications before being moved back.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/38364f7ef9e2d6fe9c4cc454fff592bfbbdefb889c3f1dc7bfa4886100a57b2e.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>When determining UltraWarm storage needs, only the size of the primary shards is considered. When querying for the list of shards in UltraWarm, both primary and replicas are listed. However, both shards are placeholders for the same, single copy of the data, which resides in Amazon S3. The durability of data in Amazon S3 eliminates the need for replicas, and Amazon S3 abstracts away any operating system or service considerations. In the hot tier, accounting for one replica, 20 GB of index uses 40 GB of storage. In the UltraWarm tier, it’s billed at 20 GB.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<strong><span>&nbsp;Add UltraWarm nodes on the cluster and move the index to it.</span></strong></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<strong><span>Store the index in an OR1 storage</span></strong>&nbsp;is incorrect because. OR1 instances keep a copy of the data in both local (Amazon EBS) and remote storage (Amazon S3). This contrasts with UltraWarm nodes, which are optimized to store data primarily in remote storage to reduce costs. This makes OR1 instances more expensive compared to UltraWarm.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Move the index in a Cold storage</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Although Cold storage is the most cost-effective for long-term storage of rarely accessed data, it requires a reattachment process to the cluster for the data to become accessible.&nbsp;This introduces delays in data availability, contradicting the requirement for data to be available immediately when accessed.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Use Index State Management (ISM) to schedule the deletion of the index data</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. ISM is a feature in Amazon OpenSearch Service that automates routine index management tasks. It can be used to move data between different storage classes (hot, UltraWarm, and cold) based on a schedule or delete stale data.&nbsp;Since there is no definite period for when to delete or continue storing the data, applying ISM isn't the right solution for this scenario.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/ultrawarm.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/ultrawarm.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/choose-the-right-storage-tier-for-your-needs-in-amazon-opensearch-service/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/choose-the-right-storage-tier-for-your-needs-in-amazon-opensearch-service/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon OpenSearch Service Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-opensearch-service/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-opensearch-service/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":49,"sourceNumber":63,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p>A multinational corporation has recently started using Amazon Redshift for its data warehousing needs. The company's database contains a large <code>sales</code> table that stores all the transactions and a smaller <code>products</code> table containing product details. The <code>sales</code> table is frequently joined with the <code>products</code> table for generating various reports.</p>\n<p>The management team has noticed that some queries are taking longer than expected to return results, especially those involving joins between the <code>sales</code> and <code>products</code> tables. The task is to optimize the performance of these queries.</p>\n<p>Which distribution style should be recommended to optimize the performance of the join queries?</p>","answers":["<span>Use the <code>KEY</code> distribution style for the <code>sales</code> table based on the <code>product_id</code> column and the <code>ALL</code> distribution style for the <code>products</code> table to replicate it across all nodes in the cluster.</span>","<span>Use the <code>EVEN</code> distribution style for both <code>sales</code> and <code>products</code> tables to distribute the rows evenly across all nodes in the cluster.</span>","<span>Use the <code>KEY</code> distribution style for both <code>sales</code> and <code>products</code> tables based on the <code>product_id</code> and <code>id</code> columns respectively to distribute the rows across the nodes in the cluster.</span>","<span>Use the <code>ALL</code> distribution style for both <code>sales</code> and <code>products</code> tables to replicate all rows across all nodes in the cluster.</span>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Amazon Redshift is a data warehousing service that makes it simple and cost-effective to analyze all your data using standard SQL. One of the key features of Redshift is its ability to handle large amounts of data and complex queries by distributing data and query execution across multiple nodes. The method by which Redshift distributes the data across the nodes is called the distribution style. There are three types of distribution styles in Redshift:&nbsp;<code>KEY</code>,&nbsp;<code>ALL</code>, and&nbsp;<code>EVEN</code>.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/b7975eb8291af7b781cd8aa89255bab1fd8f8eb4c5fa666e32cb5c936ce1468c.gif\" alt=\"\"></p>\n<p>The&nbsp;<code>KEY</code>&nbsp;distribution style distributes the data across the nodes based on the values in one column. The rows with the same value in the distribution key column are located on the same node. This is beneficial when two tables are frequently joined on the distribution key column, as it allows for fast, local joins. The&nbsp;<code>ALL</code>&nbsp;distribution style replicates the entire table on every node. This is beneficial for small tables that are frequently joined with large tables, as it allows for fast, local joins. However, it uses a lot of storage space and can slow down data loading and update operations. The&nbsp;<code>EVEN</code>&nbsp;distribution style (the default) distributes the rows evenly across all nodes without considering the join patterns. This could lead to a lot of network I/O during the join operations, which could slow down the query performance.</p>\n<p>In the given scenario, the&nbsp;<code>sales</code> table is a large table that stores all the sales transactions and the&nbsp;<code>products</code>&nbsp;table is a smaller table that contains product details. The&nbsp;<code>sales</code>&nbsp;table is frequently joined with the&nbsp;<code>products</code>&nbsp;table to generate various reports. Therefore, using the&nbsp;<code>KEY</code>&nbsp;distribution style for the&nbsp;<code>sales</code>&nbsp;table based on the&nbsp;<code>product_id</code>&nbsp;column and the&nbsp;<code>ALL</code>&nbsp;distribution style for the&nbsp;<code>products</code>&nbsp;table to replicate it across all nodes in the cluster would be beneficial. This would ensure that the rows with the same product ID are located on the same node, allowing for fast, local joins and that the entire&nbsp;<code>products</code>&nbsp;table is available on every node.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:<span> <strong>Use the&nbsp;<code>KEY</code>&nbsp;distribution style for the&nbsp;<code>sales</code>&nbsp;table based on the&nbsp;<code>product_id</code>&nbsp;column and the&nbsp;<code>ALL</code>&nbsp;distribution style for the&nbsp;<code>products</code>&nbsp;table to replicate it across all nodes in the cluster.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use the&nbsp;<code>EVEN</code>&nbsp;distribution style for both&nbsp;<code>sales</code>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<code>products</code>&nbsp;tables to distribute the rows evenly across all nodes in the cluster</strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect&nbsp;because the&nbsp;<code>EVEN</code>&nbsp;distribution style simply distributes the rows evenly across all nodes in the cluster without considering the join patterns. This could lead to a lot of network I/O during the join operations, which could slow down the query performance.</p>\n<p>The option that says:<span><strong>&nbsp;Use the&nbsp;<code>KEY</code>&nbsp;distribution style for both&nbsp;<code>sales</code>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<code>products</code>&nbsp;tables based on the&nbsp;<code>product_id</code>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<code>id</code>&nbsp;columns respectively to distribute the rows across the nodes in the cluster</strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect. While this approach can ensure related data resides on the same node, it does not consider the size disparity between the tables. For very large and smaller tables, replicating the smaller table across tables&nbsp;can further reduce the need for cross-node data movement during joins, optimizing performance more than matching <code>KEY</code> distributions alone.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Use the&nbsp;<code>ALL</code>&nbsp;distribution style for both&nbsp;<code>sales</code>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<code>products</code>&nbsp;tables to replicate all rows across all nodes in the cluster</strong></span>&nbsp;is&nbsp;incorrect. Using the&nbsp;<code>ALL</code>&nbsp;distribution style for both tables would result in a lot of storage space being used, as every row from both tables would be replicated on every node. This could also slow down the data loading and update operations. This distribution style is typically recommended for small dimension tables that are frequently joined with large fact tables.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Distributing_data.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Distributing_data.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_choosing_dist_sort.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_choosing_dist_sort.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-engineerings-advanced-table-design-playbook-distribution-styles-and-distribution-keys/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-engineerings-advanced-table-design-playbook-distribution-styles-and-distribution-keys/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":50,"sourceNumber":64,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p><span>A financial company uses an Amazon Aurora database with Database Activity Streams enabled for its loan approval system. When a loan application's status is updated in the database, different processing systems must be triggered to execute various tasks.</span></p>\n<p><span>Which option would best meet the requirements?</span></p>","answers":["Create a stored procedure or native function that invokes an AWS Lambda function upon loan status updates. Configure the Lambda function to forward the approval event to an Amazon SQS queue for subsequent processing actions of the systems.","Set up an Amazon RDS event subscription to trigger an AWS Lambda function upon loan status update. Configure the Lambda function to send the approval event to an Amazon SQS queue. Update the systems to read from the queue.","Create an Amazon EventBridge rule to detect changes in loan status within the database. Configure the rule to trigger an AWS Lambda function to send approval events to an Amazon SQS queue. Update the systems to read from the queue.","Configure the Aurora to publish messages directly on an Amazon SNS topic upon loan approval. Subscribe an AWS Lambda function to this topic. Configure the function to forward messages to an Amazon SQS queue for processing by the systems."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>You can leverage AWS Lambda functions to manage data changes. When a loan application is approved, the corresponding data needs to be removed from the current applications and forwarded to a distributed processing system. This can be achieved by invoking an AWS Lambda function from an Amazon Aurora MySQL-Compatible Edition DB cluster using a native function or a stored procedure. This approach is particularly useful when you want to integrate your Aurora MySQL database with other AWS services. For instance, you might want to capture data changes whenever a row in a table is modified in your database.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/b70b2a14ad630d108b7811e3cf408d09c00bc1093bb91f0ee450ee042b02a879.png\" alt=\"Aurora, Lambda, and SQS\"></p>\n<p>In this scenario, you can set up a trigger that activates a Lambda function whenever a loan is approved. The Lambda function can then be programmed to remove the approved loan data from the current applications in the Aurora database and send the removed loan data to an Amazon Simple Queue Service (SQS) queue. The distributed processing system can then consume the loan data from the SQS queue at its own pace, effectively achieving the requirement of forwarding approved loan data from the Aurora database to the processing system.</p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is:&nbsp;<span><strong>Create a stored procedure or native function that invokes an AWS Lambda function upon loan status updates. Configure the Lambda function to forward the approval event to an Amazon SQS queue for subsequent processing actions of the systems.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Set up an Amazon RDS event subscription to trigger an AWS Lambda function upon loan status update. Configure the Lambda function to send the approval event to an Amazon SQS queue. Update the systems to read from the queue</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. RDS Event Subscriptions can only notify you of operational events such as instance status changes. They can't monitor table-level events like inserts or updates, which the scenario requires.</p>\n<p>The option that says:&nbsp;<span><strong>Create an Amazon EventBridge rule to detect changes in loan status within the database. Configure the rule to trigger an AWS Lambda function to send approval events to an Amazon SQS queue. Update the systems to read from the queue</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. EventBridge doesn't directly monitor changes at the data level within Aurora databases. It would typically require some mechanism to emit those changes Lambda as events, which isn't natively supported in Amazon Aurora.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Configure the Aurora to publish messages directly on an Amazon SNS topic upon loan approval. Subscribe an AWS Lambda function to this topic. Configure the function to forward messages to an Amazon SQS queue for processing by the systems</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. While Amazon Aurora supports invoking AWS Lambda functions based on database activity, it's not capable of directly publishing events to SNS topics without a Lambda function acting as an intermediary.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/AuroraMySQL.Integrating.Lambda.html\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/AuroraMySQL.Integrating.Lambda.html</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/capturing-data-changes-in-amazon-aurora-using-aws-lambda/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/capturing-data-changes-in-amazon-aurora-using-aws-lambda/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon Aurora Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-aurora/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-aurora/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"},{"id":51,"sourceNumber":65,"previouslyWrong":false,"previouslyUnanswered":true,"question":"<p><span>An e-commerce company stores customer data in an Amazon Redshift cluster hosted in a private VPC. To safeguard sensitive data such as customer contact details, Amazon QuickSight enforces column-level security (CLS) during report generation.</span></p>\n<p><span>The company aims to create visual charts of sales rankings by product category and customer demographics. To achieve this, the company's data engineer must configure QuickSight to connect to the Redshift cluster securely.</span></p>\n<p><span>How can the data engineer meet the requirements in the LEAST amount of effort?</span></p>","answers":["Set up a VPC connection in QuickSight. Create inbound and outbound rules in the security groups for both services to allow traffic between them.","Create an outbound rule in the security group of the Redshift cluster to allow traffic to Quicksight’s IP address. Establish a VPN connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight and modify the network ACLs to allow traffic between them.","Create an inbound rule in the security group of the Redshift cluster to allow traffic from Quicksight’s IP address. Establish a Direct Connect connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight and modify the route tables to allow traffic between them.","Set up a VPC connection in QuickSight. Establish a VPC peering connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight’s VPC. Create inbound and outbound rules in the security groups for both services to allow traffic between them."],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>You can create a VPC connection in Amazon QuickSight to securely access private databases, such as an Amazon Redshift cluster, directly within your VPC. This setup is ideal for scenarios where your databases must be accessible only from certain IP addresses or within a secure environment, ensuring your data remains isolated from the public internet. When QuickSight connects to your VPC, it creates elastic network interfaces (ENIs) to facilitate a secure channel of communication directly to your resources, like a Redshift cluster, without traversing the public internet.</p>\n<p>You can connect Amazon QuickSight to the same VPC where your Amazon Redshift cluster is located. Once connected, you'll need to configure the security groups associated with both QuickSight and the Amazon Redshift cluster. The security groups act as virtual firewalls, controlling the traffic that can enter and leave the network interfaces.</p>\n<p>In the Redshift security group's configuration, the inbound rule must allow TCP traffic on port 5439 (the default port number of Redshift) from the QuickSight security group, enabling QuickSight to initiate connections to the Redshift cluster. The outbound rule should permit all TCP traffic to the QuickSight security group, allowing Redshift to send responses back to QuickSight.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/c5b3cd0611879e227eb94eb58ce4877b7642d1617642e7162ff74abcae20851a.png\" alt=\"Amazon Redshift Security Group\"></p>\n<p>For QuickSight's security group, the inbound rules need to allow incoming responses from Redshift’s port range, and the outbound rules must permit initiating queries to the Redshift cluster on port 5439.</p>\n<p><img src=\"exams/assets/08c668c073ee98f2a0349dee97348afac4fd638d73df55c65600e9efef1ce3ff.png\" alt=\"Amazon QuickSight Security Group\"></p>\n<p>Hence, the correct answer is: <span><strong>Set up a VPC connection in QuickSight. Create inbound and outbound rules in the security groups for both services to allow traffic between them.</strong></span></p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create an outbound rule in the security group of the Redshift cluster to allow traffic to Quicksight’s IP address. Establish a VPN connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight and modify the network ACLs to allow traffic between them</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Establishing a VPN connection is not necessary in this scenario. The use of VPNs is more applicable to scenarios where you are connecting on-premise resources to AWS resources, not for connecting AWS services within the same region.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Create an inbound rule in the security group of the Redshift cluster to allow traffic from Quicksight’s IP address. Establish a Direct Connect connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight and modify the route tables to allow traffic between them</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. Similar to the other incorrect option,&nbsp;this only introduces complexity that isn't necessary for connecting QuickSight to Redshift. Direct Connect is primarily used to create a dedicated, private connection between your on-premises data center and AWS. QuickSight, being a fully managed AWS service, does not require Direct Connect to access Redshift within AWS.</p>\n<p>The option that says: <span><strong>Set up a VPC connection in QuickSight. Establish a networking VPC peering connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight’s VPC. Create inbound and outbound rules in the security groups for both services to allow traffic between them</strong></span>&nbsp;is incorrect. While this method is technically viable, VPC peering just adds unnecessary complexity to the given requirement. Since QuickSight can connect to Redshift through a simpler VPC connection setup, introducing VPC peering is not the most straightforward approach. It would be useful in scenarios where a Redshift cluster and QuickSight need to communicate across different VPCs&nbsp;without a shared VPC connection or when cross-account VPC communication is required.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/working-with-aws-vpc.html#private-connection-to-vpc-using-quicksight\">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/working-with-aws-vpc.html#private-connection-to-vpc-using-quicksight</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/quicksight-redshift-private-connection\">https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/quicksight-redshift-private-connection</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-quicksight-deployment-models-for-cross-account-and-cross-region-access-to-amazon-redshift-and-amazon-rds/\">https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-quicksight-deployment-models-for-cross-account-and-cross-region-access-to-amazon-redshift-and-amazon-rds/</a></p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Check out this Amazon QuickSight Cheat Sheet:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-quicksight/\">https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-quicksight/</a></p>","section":"Time Mode 2 · Câu bổ sung"}]};